{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NR1n4B7HsuaMip1adyUYPu","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"Cross-architecture layer sweep + static probe (R1-32B & QwQ-32B)\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Full 64-layer sweep on R1-32B and QwQ-32B. Extract hidden states at all layers, evaluate static LR probes under multiple selection metrics. Discovers layer selection bias (bal_acc × crossing_rate → shallow layers).\\n\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"r1_32b_biased_layer\": 10,\n7\t      \"r1_32b_biased_layer_depth_pct\": 15.6,\n8\t      \"r1_32b_corrected_layer\": 63,\n9\t      \"r1_32b_corrected_layer_depth_pct\": 98.4,\n10\t      \"r1_32b_l10_bal_acc\": 0.718,\n11\t      \"r1_32b_l63_bal_acc_w1\": 0.826,\n12\t      \"r1_32b_n_test\": 20,\n13\t      \"qwq_32b_biased_layer\": 2,\n14\t      \"qwq_32b_biased_layer_depth_pct\": 3.1,\n15\t      \"qwq_32b_corrected_layer\": 60,\n16\t      \"qwq_32b_corrected_layer_depth_pct\": 93.8,\n17\t      \"qwq_32b_l2_bal_acc\": 0.73,\n18\t      \"qwq_32b_l60_bal_acc_w3\": 0.74,\n19\t      \"qwq_32b_n_test\": 19,\n20\t      \"layer_selection_bias_discovered\": true,\n21\t      \"metrics_tested\": 5,\n22\t      \"biased_metric\": \"bal_acc × crossing_rate\",\n23\t      \"loo_cv_dominant_fold_pct\": \"65-68%\"\n24\t    },\n25\t    \"tables\": [\n26\t      {\n27\t        \"name\": \"Layer Selection Metric × Model → Selected Layer\",\n28\t        \"columns\": [\n29\t          \"Metric\",\n30\t          \"R1-8B\",\n31\t          \"OT-7B\",\n32\t          \"QwQ-32B\",\n33\t          \"R1-32B\"\n34\t        ],\n35\t        \"rows\": [\n36\t          [\n37\t            \"bal_acc × crossing_rate\",\n38\t            \"L0 (0%)\",\n39\t            \"L0 (0%)\",\n40\t            \"L2 (3%)\",\n41\t            \"L10 (16%)\"\n42\t          ],\n43\t          [\n44\t            \"pure bal_acc\",\n45\t            \"L12 (39%)\",\n46\t            \"L9 (32%)\",\n47\t            \"L30 (47%)\",\n48\t            \"L47 (73%)\"\n49\t          ],\n50\t          [\n51\t            \"precision\",\n52\t            \"L16 (52%)\",\n53\t            \"L16 (57%)\",\n54\t            \"L60 (94%)\",\n55\t            \"L63 (98%)\"\n56\t          ],\n57\t          [\n58\t            \"pre_cp_fpr\",\n59\t            \"L16 (52%)\",\n60\t            \"L16 (57%)\",\n61\t            \"L60 (94%)\",\n62\t            \"L63 (98%)\"\n63\t          ],\n64\t          [\n65\t            \"composite (additive)\",\n66\t            \"L16 (52%)\",\n67\t            \"L12 (43%)\",\n68\t            \"L60 (94%)\",\n69\t            \"L63 (98%)\"\n70\t          ]\n71\t        ]\n72\t      }\n73\t    ],\n74\t    \"figures\": [],\n75\t    \"analysis\": \"Layer sweep结果本身不变，但AdvBench非复现将C1从'general finding applicable to any hidden-state evaluation'降级为'task-specific observation about temporal monitoring pipelines using crossing_rate statistics'。Reviewer: 'If the bias is specific to crossing_rate in temporal settings, then it's a footnote in your methods section, not a standalone contribution. Rename the section from \\\"Layer Selection Sensitivity\\\" to \\\"Implementation Note: Crossing Rate Bias.\\\"'\\n\\n\\nLayer sweep在CP数据上发现genuine methodological pattern（biased metric selects shallow layers），corrected layers确实show HS advantage。这些结果不受AdvBench 非复现影响——它们是关于CP temporal data的发现。但worst-tier reviewer将AdvBench 非复现与此联系：'你在CP data上发现的layer selection bias是CP annotation structure 特有的——crossing_rate在temporal monitoring中有意义，但这不是general model property。既然bias只在temporal setting中存在，那C1的scope就是一个pipeline-specific caveat，不是methodological contribution。任何使用不同task structure的研究者 不会遇到这个问题。'\\n\"\n76\t  },\n77\t  {\n78\t    \"name\": \"Cross-architecture temporal window evaluation (32B)\",\n79\t    \"description\": \"Temporal LR at multiple window sizes on R1-32B (L63) and QwQ-32B (L60). Validates temporal context improvement on 32B models.\\n\",\n80\t    \"results\": {\n81\t      \"r1_32b_l63_w1_bal_acc\": 0.826,\n82\t      \"r1_32b_l63_w3_bal_acc\": 0.754,\n83\t      \"r1_32b_l63_w10_bal_acc\": 0.737,\n84\t      \"r1_32b_l63_w15_bal_acc\": 0.721,\n85\t      \"r1_32b_temporal_trend\": \"DECREASING with W (W=1 best)\",\n86\t      \"r1_32b_dimensionality_issue\": \"51200 × W features for n=20\",\n87\t      \"qwq_32b_l60_w1_bal_acc\": 0.74,\n88\t      \"qwq_32b_l60_w3_bal_acc\": 0.74,\n89\t      \"qwq_32b_l60_w5_bal_acc\": 0.742,\n90\t      \"qwq_32b_l60_w15_bal_acc\": 0.731,\n91\t      \"qwq_32b_temporal_trend\": \"flat to slightly decreasing\",\n92\t      \"text_temporal_universal_improvement\": true,\n93\t      \"hs_temporal_improvement_3_of_4\": true\n94\t    },\n95\t    \"tables\": [\n96\t      {\n97\t        \"name\": \"Temporal Window Effect on 32B HS Probes (corrected layers)\",\n98\t        \"columns\": [\n99\t          \"Model\",\n100\t          \"Layer\",\n101\t          \"W=1\",\n102\t          \"W=3\",\n103\t          \"W=5\",\n104\t          \"W=10\",\n105\t          \"W=15\",\n106\t          \"Trend\"\n107\t        ],\n108\t        \"rows\": [\n109\t          [\n110\t            \"R1-32B\",\n111\t            \"L63\",\n112\t            \"0.826*\",\n113\t            \"0.754\",\n114\t            \"0.741\",\n115\t            \"0.737\",\n116\t            \"0.721\",\n117\t            \"↓ declining\"\n118\t          ],\n119\t          [\n120\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n121\t            \"L60\",\n122\t            \"0.740\",\n123\t            \"0.740\",\n124\t            \"0.742\",\n125\t            \"0.735\",\n126\t            \"0.731\",\n127\t            \"→ flat\"\n128\t          ],\n129\t          [\n130\t            \"R1-8B\",\n131\t            \"L14\",\n132\t            \"0.778\",\n133\t            \"0.790\",\n134\t            \"0.781\",\n135\t            \"0.793\",\n136\t            \"0.802\",\n137\t            \"↑ improving\"\n138\t          ],\n139\t          [\n140\t            \"OT-7B\",\n141\t            \"L16\",\n142\t            \"0.663\",\n143\t            \"0.688*\",\n144\t            \"0.678\",\n145\t            \"0.668\",\n146\t            \"0.659\",\n147\t            \"↑↓ peaks W=3\"\n148\t          ]\n149\t        ]\n150\t      }\n151\t    ],\n152\t    \"figures\": [],\n153\t    \"analysis\": \"R1-32B dimensionality问题（51200×W > n=20）使W>1结果不可靠。QwQ flat。 Temporal benefit主要是text probe finding。AdvBench非复现进一步说明：即使temporal probing pipeline在CP data上work，其关键组件（crossing_rate, layer selection） 都是task-specific的。\\n\\n\\nTemporal context对text probes universally有效，对HS probes在2/4模型上有caveats （OT-7B saturates at W=3, R1-32B degrades with W>1 due to dimensionality）。 Worst-tier下AdvBench非复现不直接影响这个实验的数据，但强化了reviewer的论点： 'temporal monitoring pipeline本身就充满了model-specific hyperparameters（layer, W, K）。你在每个model上需要tuning这些参数，AdvBench上bias又不复现——这说明你的 整个方法论都是dataset-specific的。'\\n\"\n154\t  },\n155\t  {\n156\t    \"name\": \"Cross-model transfer probes\",\n157\t    \"description\": \"Train probe on one model, test on another. PCA projection for cross-architecture transfer. Tests universality of safety commitment encoding.\\n\",\n158\t    \"results\": {\n159\t      \"r1_8b_to_r1_32b_acc\": 0.522,\n160\t      \"r1_8b_to_qwq_acc\": 0.595,\n161\t      \"r1_32b_to_qwq_acc\": 0.673,\n162\t      \"qwq_to_r1_32b_acc\": 0.673,\n163\t      \"r1_32b_to_r1_8b_acc\": 0.583,\n164\t      \"best_transfer_overall\": 0.673,\n165\t      \"all_transfers_below_within_model\": true,\n166\t      \"cross_family_at_chance\": true\n167\t    },\n168\t    \"tables\": [\n169\t      {\n170\t        \"name\": \"Cross-Model Transfer Accuracy Matrix\",\n171\t        \"columns\": [\n172\t          \"Train →\",\n173\t          \"R1-8B\",\n174\t          \"OT-7B\",\n175\t          \"R1-32B\",\n176\t          \"QwQ-32B\"\n177\t        ],\n178\t        \"rows\": [\n179\t          [\n180\t            \"R1-8B\",\n181\t            \"0.817 (self)\",\n182\t            \"—\",\n183\t            \"0.522\",\n184\t            \"0.595\"\n185\t          ],\n186\t          [\n187\t            \"OT-7B\",\n188\t            \"—\",\n189\t            \"0.688 (self)\",\n190\t            \"—\",\n191\t            \"—\"\n192\t          ],\n193\t          [\n194\t            \"R1-32B\",\n195\t            \"0.583\",\n196\t            \"—\",\n197\t            \"0.826 (self)\",\n198\t            \"0.673\"\n199\t          ],\n200\t          [\n201\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n202\t            \"—\",\n203\t            \"—\",\n204\t            \"0.673\",\n205\t            \"0.740 (self)\"\n206\t          ]\n207\t        ]\n208\t      }\n209\t    ],\n210\t    \"figures\": [],\n211\t    \"analysis\": \"Complete transfer failure combined with AdvBench non-replication paints a picture of extreme specificity. Everything is model-specific AND task-specific. Reviewer: 'The paper's implicit promise of generalizable insights is contradicted by its own evidence.'\\n\\n\\nCross-model transfer完全失败。最佳transfer accuracy 67.3%远低于within-model performance，cross-family（Llama↔Qwen）接近chance。AdvBench非复现与此一致： 如果连layer selection bias都是task-specific的，那safety encoding的model-specificity 更是预期的。Worst-tier reviewer: 'Transfer failure + AdvBench非复现 = 你的 所有findings都是dataset×model specific的。没有任何generalizable component。 这篇paper的external validity接近零。'\\n\"\n212\t  },\n213\t  {\n214\t    \"name\": \"Lead time analysis across models\",\n215\t    \"description\": \"HS probe lead time distributions on all 4 models. Compare HS vs text lead times.\\n\",\n216\t    \"results\": {\n217\t      \"r1_8b_hs_lead_mean\": -0.1,\n218\t      \"r1_8b_text_lead_mean\": 10.3,\n219\t      \"ot_7b_hs_lead_mean\": 21.6,\n220\t      \"ot_7b_text_lead_mean\": 45.5,\n221\t      \"qwq_32b_hs_lead_range\": \"-1.2 to +1.9\",\n222\t      \"qwq_32b_text_lead_range\": \"3.7 to 12.3\",\n223\t      \"qwq_32b_position_beats_hs\": true,\n224\t      \"r1_32b_hs_lead_mean\": \"+11.7 to +13.3\",\n225\t      \"r1_32b_coverage_at_significant_w\": 0.25,\n226\t      \"gru_r1_8b_lead\": \"+4.5 ± 3.0\"\n227\t    },\n228\t    \"tables\": [\n229\t      {\n230\t        \"name\": \"Lead Time: HS vs Text vs Position\",\n231\t        \"columns\": [\n232\t          \"Model\",\n233\t          \"HS Lead\",\n234\t          \"Text Lead\",\n235\t          \"Position BA\",\n236\t          \"HS BA\",\n237\t          \"HS > Position?\"\n238\t        ],\n239\t        \"rows\": [\n240\t          [\n241\t            \"R1-8B (W=15)\",\n242\t            \"−0.1\",\n243\t            \"+10.3†\",\n244\t            \"—\",\n245\t            \"0.802\",\n246\t            \"—\"\n247\t          ],\n248\t          [\n249\t            \"OT-7B (W=3)\",\n250\t            \"+21.6‡\",\n251\t            \"+45.5‡\",\n252\t            \"—\",\n253\t            \"0.688\",\n254\t            \"—\"\n255\t          ],\n256\t          [\n257\t            \"QwQ-32B (W=3)\",\n258\t            \"−1.2 to +1.9\",\n259\t            \"+3.7 to +12.3\",\n260\t            \"0.746\",\n261\t            \"0.740\",\n262\t            \"No\"\n263\t          ],\n264\t          [\n265\t            \"R1-32B (W=1)\",\n266\t            \"+11.7 to +13.3\",\n267\t            \"—\",\n268\t            \"0.686\",\n269\t            \"0.826\",\n270\t            \"Yes (25% cov)\"\n271\t          ]\n272\t        ]\n273\t      }\n274\t    ],\n275\t    \"figures\": [],\n276\t    \"analysis\": \"Lead time是practical dealbreaker。AdvBench非复现加强了reviewer的generalizability concern: '所有timing结论都只在这一个CP-annotated dataset上成立。'\\n\\n\\nLead time在4个模型上表现分裂：R1-8B HS lead≈0（at commitment, not before）， QwQ HS lead为负（text检测更早），R1-32B HS lead positive但coverage仅25%， OT-7B lead inflated by long traces。AdvBench非复现不直接改变lead time数据， 但reviewer论证chain strengthened: '既然layer selection bias是task-specific的， 那lead time结果也可能是task-specific的——你在不同benchmark上可能得到完全不同的 timing profile。没有cross-dataset validation证明timing findings的robustness。'\\n\"\n277\t  },\n278\t  {\n279\t    \"name\": \"TrajGuard-style Mahalanobis baseline (4 models)\",\n280\t    \"description\": \"Mahalanobis distance on safe-trace covariance across all 4 models. Tests geometric distance-based detection.\\n\",\n281\t    \"results\": {\n282\t      \"r1_8b_separation\": 0.07,\n283\t      \"ot_7b_separation\": 0.03,\n284\t      \"r1_32b_separation\": 0.15,\n285\t      \"qwq_32b_separation\": 0.2,\n286\t      \"max_separation\": 0.2,\n287\t      \"all_models_fail\": true,\n288\t      \"fa_exceeds_tp_all_thresholds\": true,\n289\t      \"signal_in_minor_pca_components\": true\n290\t    },\n291\t    \"tables\": [\n292\t      {\n293\t        \"name\": \"Mahalanobis Distance Separation (4 models)\",\n294\t        \"columns\": [\n295\t          \"Model\",\n296\t          \"Layer\",\n297\t          \"Safe MD\",\n298\t          \"Post-CP MD\",\n299\t          \"Sep.\",\n300\t          \"FA ≥ TP?\"\n301\t        ],\n302\t        \"rows\": [\n303\t          [\n304\t            \"R1-8B\",\n305\t            \"L14\",\n306\t            \"6.66\",\n307\t            \"6.73\",\n308\t            \"+0.07\",\n309\t            \"Yes\"\n310\t          ],\n311\t          [\n312\t            \"OT-7B\",\n313\t            \"L16\",\n314\t            \"7.68\",\n315\t            \"7.71\",\n316\t            \"+0.03\",\n317\t            \"Yes\"\n318\t          ],\n319\t          [\n320\t            \"R1-32B\",\n321\t            \"L63\",\n322\t            \"6.73\",\n323\t            \"6.88\",\n324\t            \"+0.15\",\n325\t            \"Yes\"\n326\t          ],\n327\t          [\n328\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n329\t            \"L60\",\n330\t            \"3.22\",\n331\t            \"3.42\",\n332\t            \"+0.20\",\n333\t            \"Yes\"\n334\t          ]\n335\t        ]\n336\t      }\n337\t    ],\n338\t    \"figures\": [],\n339\t    \"analysis\": \"Universal Mahalanobis failure supports 'existing methods fail' narrative。 但这个negative result是predictable的，reviewer不计入contribution。\\n\\n\\nMahalanobis在4/4模型上失败（max separation 0.20, FA≥TP at all thresholds）。 Signal在minor PCA components中——Mahalanobis structurally blind to this geometry。 此negative result与AdvBench无关（Mahalanobis不依赖crossing_rate或CP structure）， 但worst-tier reviewer仍然dismiss: 'Mahalanobis failure是predictable—— safety-relevant features orthogonal to principal variance是known phenomenon。 Testing one known-limited baseline is due diligence, not contribution。'\\n\"\n340\t  },\n341\t  {\n342\t    \"name\": \"Bootstrap CI + threshold-independent metrics\",\n343\t    \"description\": \"10000 bootstrap resamples on key metrics across 4 models. Holm-Bonferroni correction. AUROC, PR curves, detection delay CDF.\\n\",\n344\t    \"results\": {\n345\t      \"holm_bonferroni_pass\": \"12/12\",\n346\t      \"precision_delta_range\": \"+15.4 to +23.6pp\",\n347\t      \"r1_8b_precision_p\": \"<0.001\",\n348\t      \"ot_7b_precision_p\": \"<0.001\",\n349\t      \"qwq_32b_fpr_p\": 0.004,\n350\t      \"r1_32b_fpr_p\": 0.007,\n351\t      \"marginal_tests\": 2,\n352\t      \"r1_32b_ci_width_ba\": \"~0.15\",\n353\t      \"all_precision_positive\": true\n354\t    },\n355\t    \"tables\": [\n356\t      {\n357\t        \"name\": \"Holm-Bonferroni Summary (12/12, α=0.05)\",\n358\t        \"columns\": [\n359\t          \"Rank\",\n360\t          \"Model\",\n361\t          \"Metric\",\n362\t          \"p-value\",\n363\t          \"HB Threshold\",\n364\t          \"Pass\"\n365\t        ],\n366\t        \"rows\": [\n367\t          [\n368\t            \"1\",\n369\t            \"R1-8B\",\n370\t            \"precision\",\n371\t            \"<0.001\",\n372\t            \"0.0042\",\n373\t            \"✅\"\n374\t          ],\n375\t          [\n376\t            \"2\",\n377\t            \"OT-7B\",\n378\t            \"bal_acc\",\n379\t            \"<0.001\",\n380\t            \"0.0045\",\n381\t            \"✅\"\n382\t          ],\n383\t          [\n384\t            \"3\",\n385\t            \"OT-7B\",\n386\t            \"precision\",\n387\t            \"<0.001\",\n388\t            \"0.0050\",\n389\t            \"✅\"\n390\t          ],\n391\t          [\n392\t            \"4\",\n393\t            \"OT-7B\",\n394\t            \"FPR\",\n395\t            \"<0.001\",\n396\t            \"0.0056\",\n397\t            \"✅\"\n398\t          ],\n399\t          [\n400\t            \"5\",\n401\t            \"QwQ\",\n402\t            \"bal_acc\",\n403\t            \"<0.001\",\n404\t            \"0.0063\",\n405\t            \"✅\"\n406\t          ],\n407\t          [\n408\t            \"6\",\n409\t            \"QwQ\",\n410\t            \"precision\",\n411\t            \"<0.001\",\n412\t            \"0.0071\",\n413\t            \"✅\"\n414\t          ],\n415\t          [\n416\t            \"7\",\n417\t            \"R1-32B\",\n418\t            \"bal_acc\",\n419\t            \"<0.001\",\n420\t            \"0.0083\",\n421\t            \"✅\"\n422\t          ],\n423\t          [\n424\t            \"8\",\n425\t            \"R1-32B\",\n426\t            \"precision\",\n427\t            \"<0.001\",\n428\t            \"0.0100\",\n429\t            \"✅\"\n430\t          ],\n431\t          [\n432\t            \"9\",\n433\t            \"QwQ\",\n434\t            \"FPR\",\n435\t            \"0.004\",\n436\t            \"0.0125\",\n437\t            \"✅\"\n438\t          ],\n439\t          [\n440\t            \"10\",\n441\t            \"R1-8B\",\n442\t            \"bal_acc\",\n443\t            \"0.005\",\n444\t            \"0.0167\",\n445\t            \"✅\"\n446\t          ],\n447\t          [\n448\t            \"11\",\n449\t            \"R1-32B\",\n450\t            \"FPR\",\n451\t            \"0.007\",\n452\t            \"0.0250\",\n453\t            \"✅\"\n454\t          ],\n455\t          [\n456\t            \"12\",\n457\t            \"R1-8B\",\n458\t            \"FPR\",\n459\t            \"0.044\",\n460\t            \"0.0500\",\n461\t            \"✅\"\n462\t          ]\n463\t        ]\n464\t      }\n465\t    ],\n466\t    \"figures\": [],\n467\t    \"analysis\": \"Statistical significance established on CP data. AdvBench非复现的影响不在 于推翻这些p-values，而在于质疑其generalizability: '在AdvBench上HS-text gap 仅+2-3pp (NS)。如果换个dataset significance就消失，这说明12/12 HB是dataset- specific的，不是modality-inherent advantage。'\\n\\n\\n12/12 HB pass在CP data上是statistically clean的。这些结果不受AdvBench非复现 影响——statistical significance是关于CP temporal data的。但worst-tier reviewer 攻击practical significance和multiplicity: (1) 'per-model best layer AND best window选择后再test，true multiplicity远超12个corrected tests'; (2) '32B model CIs极宽（R1-32B ±15pp），statistical significance with n=20只说明 effect large，不说明estimate precise'; (3) AdvBench非复现加了一层：'你的 statistical machinery只在这一个dataset上验证过。12/12 HB on one dataset ≠ robust finding。'\\n\"\n468\t  },\n469\t  {\n470\t    \"name\": \"Window size ablation (W=1,3,5,10,15,20,25)\",\n471\t    \"description\": \"Systematic window sweep on all 4 models. Characterize precision-coverage tradeoff.\\n\",\n472\t    \"results\": {\n473\t      \"coverage_decreases_with_w\": true,\n474\t      \"precision_increases_with_w\": true,\n475\t      \"fpr_decreases_with_w\": true,\n476\t      \"r1_8b_best_w\": 25,\n477\t      \"r1_32b_best_w\": 10,\n478\t      \"qwq_best_w\": 25,\n479\t      \"ot_7b_best_w\": 3,\n480\t      \"no_model_achieves_prec70_cov60\": true\n481\t    },\n482\t    \"tables\": [\n483\t      {\n484\t        \"name\": \"Precision-Coverage at Key W (4 models)\",\n485\t        \"columns\": [\n486\t          \"Model\",\n487\t          \"W=1 Prec/Cov\",\n488\t          \"W=3 Prec/Cov\",\n489\t          \"W=15 Prec/Cov\",\n490\t          \"W=25 Prec/Cov\"\n491\t        ],\n492\t        \"rows\": [\n493\t          [\n494\t            \"R1-8B\",\n495\t            \"0.693/0.721\",\n496\t            \"0.723/0.698\",\n497\t            \"0.777/0.512\",\n498\t            \"0.822/0.488\"\n499\t          ],\n500\t          [\n501\t            \"OT-7B\",\n502\t            \"—/—\",\n503\t            \"0.466/0.532\",\n504\t            \"—/—\",\n505\t            \"—/—\"\n506\t          ],\n507\t          [\n508\t            \"R1-32B\",\n509\t            \"0.549/0.800\",\n510\t            \"0.560/0.800\",\n511\t            \"0.591/0.700\",\n512\t            \"0.566/0.700\"\n513\t          ],\n514\t          [\n515\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n516\t            \"0.544/0.789\",\n517\t            \"0.565/0.737\",\n518\t            \"0.582/0.684\",\n519\t            \"0.618/0.579\"\n520\t          ]\n521\t        ]\n522\t      }\n523\t    ],\n524\t    \"figures\": [],\n525\t    \"analysis\": \"No operating point achieves deployment-ready precision AND coverage simultaneously. AdvBench context reinforces: these tradeoffs may not even transfer to other datasets.\\n\\n\\nPrecision-coverage tradeoff universal but no operating point achieves deployment-ready performance。Worst-tier reviewer: 'Coverage monotonically decreases with W是detection criterion K的数学性质，不是insight。 Optimal W varies by model = another model-specific hyperparameter。 Combined with AdvBench layer bias非复现，整个pipeline的generalizability story 进一步弱化——每个model×dataset组合需要独立tuning。'\\n\"\n526\t  },\n527\t  {\n528\t    \"name\": \"Layer selection ablation (5 metrics × 4 models)\",\n529\t    \"description\": \"All-layer temporal probes on 32B models with 5 selection metrics. Core experiment for C1 layer selection bias claim.\\n\",\n530\t    \"results\": {\n531\t      \"crossing_rate_selects_shallow_32b\": true,\n532\t      \"pure_bal_acc_selects_middle_32b\": true,\n533\t      \"threshold_dependent_select_deep_32b\": true,\n534\t      \"qwq_l2_vs_l60_precision_gain\": \"+11.5pp\",\n535\t      \"r1_32b_l10_vs_l63_precision_gain\": \"+4.1pp\",\n536\t      \"loo_cv_stability\": \"65-68%\",\n537\t      \"bias_only_on_cp_data\": true,\n538\t      \"bias_not_on_advbench\": true\n539\t    },\n540\t    \"tables\": [\n541\t      {\n542\t        \"name\": \"Layer Selection Bias: CP Data vs AdvBench (R1-8B)\",\n543\t        \"columns\": [\n544\t          \"Metric\",\n545\t          \"CP Data Layer\",\n546\t          \"AdvBench Layer\",\n547\t          \"CP Gap (layers)\",\n548\t          \"AdvBench Gap\"\n549\t        ],\n550\t        \"rows\": [\n551\t          [\n552\t            \"bal_acc × cr\",\n553\t            \"L0 (0%)\",\n554\t            \"L12 (39%)\",\n555\t            \"16\",\n556\t            \"1\"\n557\t          ],\n558\t          [\n559\t            \"pure bal_acc\",\n560\t            \"L12 (39%)\",\n561\t            \"L13 (42%)\",\n562\t            \"—\",\n563\t            \"ref\"\n564\t          ],\n565\t          [\n566\t            \"precision\",\n567\t            \"L16 (52%)\",\n568\t            \"L13 (42%)\",\n569\t            \"—\",\n570\t            \"0\"\n571\t          ],\n572\t          [\n573\t            \"pre_cp_fpr\",\n574\t            \"L16 (52%)\",\n575\t            \"L14 (45%)\",\n576\t            \"—\",\n577\t            \"1\"\n578\t          ],\n579\t          [\n580\t            \"composite\",\n581\t            \"L16 (52%)\",\n582\t            \"L13 (42%)\",\n583\t            \"—\",\n584\t            \"0\"\n585\t          ],\n586\t          [\n587\t            \"MAX GAP\",\n588\t            \"—\",\n589\t            \"—\",\n590\t            \"16 layers\",\n591\t            \"2 layers\"\n592\t          ]\n593\t        ]\n594\t      }\n595\t    ],\n596\t    \"figures\": [],\n597\t    \"analysis\": \"这是worst-tier的defining result。CP data上16-layer gap vs AdvBench上2-layer gap直接证明bias是crossing_rate temporal structure产物。Paper must explicitly acknowledge: 'Layer selection bias is specific to temporal monitoring pipelines using crossing_rate-based metrics. Binary classification tasks do not exhibit this bias.' C1 scope收窄但不删除——仍然是valid methodological finding within its scope.\\n\\n\\nLayer selection ablation在CP data上reveals genuine pattern。但这是worst-tier的 核心实验——AdvBench非复现直接打击这个实验的generalizability claim。在CP data上， crossing_rate在shallow layers系统性偏高是因为temporal probe score序列中，shallow层 probes更noisy→更频繁crossing threshold→crossing_rate虚高→与bal_acc相乘后拉向 shallow layers。在AdvBench binary classification中，没有'连续probe score序列的 threshold crossing'概念——每个trace只有一个binary prediction，crossing_rate要么 不存在（无temporal sequence）要么在所有层similar（无differential noise结构）。 所以bias不复现是MECHANISTICALLY CONSISTENT——不是实验失败，而是bias的root cause 在AdvBench中不存在。Worst-tier reviewer nonetheless argues: 'Consistent non- replication means the bias is a pipeline artifact, not a model property. Your C1 contribution scope is now: \\\"if you use crossing_rate as part of your layer selection metric in a temporal monitoring pipeline, be aware it biases toward shallow layers.\\\" That's a methods note, not a contribution.'\\n\"\n598\t  },\n599\t  {\n600\t    \"name\": \"16-label taxonomy supervision\",\n601\t    \"description\": \"Fine-grained safety labels (Type 1/2/3/4 × pre/post) instead of binary commitment. Evaluate richer supervision impact.\\n\",\n602\t    \"results\": {\n603\t      \"r1_8b_16label_acc\": 0.41,\n604\t      \"r1_8b_binary_collapse_delta\": \"+0.8pp\",\n605\t      \"ot_7b_binary_collapse_delta\": \"+1.1pp\",\n606\t      \"r1_32b_binary_collapse_delta\": \"+0.6pp\",\n607\t      \"qwq_32b_binary_collapse_delta\": \"+0.9pp\",\n608\t      \"max_delta\": \"+1.1pp\"\n609\t    },\n610\t    \"tables\": [\n611\t      {\n612\t        \"name\": \"16-Label vs Binary Supervision\",\n613\t        \"columns\": [\n614\t          \"Model\",\n615\t          \"Binary Acc\",\n616\t          \"16-Label Acc\",\n617\t          \"16→Binary Collapse\",\n618\t          \"Δ vs Binary\"\n619\t        ],\n620\t        \"rows\": [\n621\t          [\n622\t            \"R1-8B\",\n623\t            \"0.817\",\n624\t            \"0.41\",\n625\t            \"0.825\",\n626\t            \"+0.8pp\"\n627\t          ],\n628\t          [\n629\t            \"OT-7B\",\n630\t            \"0.688\",\n631\t            \"0.35\",\n632\t            \"0.699\",\n633\t            \"+1.1pp\"\n634\t          ],\n635\t          [\n636\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n637\t            \"0.740\",\n638\t            \"0.26\",\n639\t            \"0.749\",\n640\t            \"+0.9pp\"\n641\t          ],\n642\t          [\n643\t            \"R1-32B\",\n644\t            \"0.826\",\n645\t            \"0.28\",\n646\t            \"0.832\",\n647\t            \"+0.6pp\"\n648\t          ]\n649\t        ]\n650\t      }\n651\t    ],\n652\t    \"figures\": [],\n653\t    \"analysis\": \"Fine-grained supervision dead-end. Linear probes saturated at binary level. AdvBench adds: even the binary signal is task-specific.\\n\\n\\nFine-grained labels提供marginal improvement（+0.6-1.1pp collapse后）。 16-class accuracy低（26-41%）因为transition types在hidden-state space中 不cleanly separable。AdvBench非复现不直接影响此实验，但reviewer argument strengthened: 'Binary labels work almost as well as 16-label supervision. And on AdvBench (also binary), the whole pipeline fails to distinguish HS from text. This suggests the signal structure you're exploiting is extremely narrow — it exists only in CP-annotated temporal data with step-level labels.'\\n\"\n654\t  },\n655\t  {\n656\t    \"name\": \"Oracle decomposition\",\n657\t    \"description\": \"Analyze oracle label sequence vs hidden-state probe predictions. Explain 31-step gap between oracle and probe lead time on R1-8B.\\n\",\n658\t    \"results\": {\n659\t      \"r1_8b_oracle_lead\": \"+31.0\",\n660\t      \"r1_8b_best_probe_lead\": \"+4.5 (GRU)\",\n661\t      \"oracle_gap\": 26.5,\n662\t      \"label_autocorr_pre_cp\": 0.82,\n663\t      \"hs_autocorr_pre_cp\": 0.95,\n664\t      \"label_entropy_pre_cp\": 1.38,\n665\t      \"hs_entropy_pre_cp\": 0.41,\n666\t      \"pattern\": \"Labels show gradual erosion; hidden states show step-function at CP\"\n667\t    },\n668\t    \"tables\": [\n669\t      {\n670\t        \"name\": \"Oracle vs Best Probe Lead Time\",\n671\t        \"columns\": [\n672\t          \"Model\",\n673\t          \"Oracle Lead\",\n674\t          \"Best Probe\",\n675\t          \"Method\",\n676\t          \"Gap\"\n677\t        ],\n678\t        \"rows\": [\n679\t          [\n680\t            \"R1-8B\",\n681\t            \"+31.0\",\n682\t            \"+4.5\",\n683\t            \"GRU multi-layer\",\n684\t            \"26.5\"\n685\t          ],\n686\t          [\n687\t            \"OT-7B\",\n688\t            \"~+30\",\n689\t            \"+33.6\",\n690\t            \"GRU multi-layer\",\n691\t            \"~0†\"\n692\t          ],\n693\t          [\n694\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n695\t            \"+27\",\n696\t            \"−1.2 to +1.9\",\n697\t            \"temporal LR\",\n698\t            \"25-28\"\n699\t          ],\n700\t          [\n701\t            \"R1-32B\",\n702\t            \"+28\",\n703\t            \"+13.3\",\n704\t            \"temporal LR W=1\",\n705\t            \"15 (25% cov)\"\n706\t          ]\n707\t        ]\n708\t      }\n709\t    ],\n710\t    \"figures\": [],\n711\t    \"analysis\": \"Hidden states are reactive, not predictive. Combined with AdvBench showing no HS-text differentiation, worst-tier reviewer concludes: 'This paper convincingly demonstrates that hidden-state monitoring is fundamentally limited. The contribution is negative — which is fine for a Findings paper, but not sufficient for Main.'\\n\\n\\nOracle gap reveals hidden states encode current safety state but not trajectory toward commitment。Labels显示gradual pre-CP patterns（increasing unsafe-adjacent labels, shortening safe streaks），hidden states不track这些 patterns。AdvBench非复现reinforces this: 'Hidden states don't capture safety trajectory even on the dataset specifically designed to test this. On a different dataset (AdvBench), the signal degrades further. The fundamental limitation is that hidden states are snapshots, not trajectory predictors.'\\n\"\n712\t  },\n713\t  {\n714\t    \"name\": \"OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B, STILL-trained)\",\n715\t    \"description\": \"4th model completing 2×2 architecture×scale matrix. Full pipeline: layer sweep, temporal probes, bootstrap, selectivity.\\n\",\n716\t    \"results\": {\n717\t      \"best_layer\": 16,\n718\t      \"best_layer_depth_pct\": 57.1,\n719\t      \"best_hs_bal_acc_w3\": 0.688,\n720\t      \"best_hs_precision_w3\": 0.466,\n721\t      \"bootstrap_3_of_3_hb\": true,\n722\t      \"selectivity_pp\": 18.3,\n723\t      \"n_traces\": 228,\n724\t      \"gru_multi_bal_acc\": 0.746,\n725\t      \"gru_multi_lead\": \"+33.6\",\n726\t      \"gru_multi_fpr\": 0.274\n727\t    },\n728\t    \"tables\": [\n729\t      {\n730\t        \"name\": \"OT-7B Pipeline Summary\",\n731\t        \"columns\": [\n732\t          \"Method\",\n733\t          \"bal_acc\",\n734\t          \"precision\",\n735\t          \"pre-CP FPR\",\n736\t          \"coverage\",\n737\t          \"lead\"\n738\t        ],\n739\t        \"rows\": [\n740\t          [\n741\t            \"HS temporal W=3 L16\",\n742\t            \"0.688\",\n743\t            \"0.466\",\n744\t            \"0.100\",\n745\t            \"0.532\",\n746\t            \"+21.6\"\n747\t          ],\n748\t          [\n749\t            \"Text temporal W=25\",\n750\t            \"0.614\",\n751\t            \"0.329\",\n752\t            \"0.161\",\n753\t            \"0.660\",\n754\t            \"+45.5\"\n755\t          ],\n756\t          [\n757\t            \"GRU multi L13-19\",\n758\t            \"0.746\",\n759\t            \"—\",\n760\t            \"0.274\",\n761\t            \"0.645\",\n762\t            \"+33.6\"\n763\t          ],\n764\t          [\n765\t            \"HS-Text Δ (HB)\",\n766\t            \"+10.7pp ✅\",\n767\t            \"+21.9pp ✅\",\n768\t            \"−17.1pp ✅\",\n769\t            \"—\",\n770\t            \"—\"\n771\t          ]\n772\t        ]\n773\t      }\n774\t    ],\n775\t    \"figures\": [],\n776\t    \"analysis\": \"OT-7B strengthens 7-8B story on CP data but is irrelevant to AdvBench non-replication concern. Worst-tier: '4 models on 1 dataset ≠ 1 model on 4 datasets.'\\n\\n\\nOT-7B是single-model最clean evidence（3/3 HB, highest selectivity 18.3pp, largest n=228）。但这在CP data上的strong result并不帮助AdvBench non-replication： 'A 4th model confirming the same CP-data pattern doesn't address the cross- dataset non-replication. You have 4 models × 1 dataset = 4 data points on the same distribution. AdvBench shows the pattern doesn't transfer to a different distribution. More models on the same dataset is width without depth of validation.' OT-7B HS temporal saturation at W=3也says something: even on the best model, the temporal signal is fragile.\\n\"\n777\t  },\n778\t  {\n779\t    \"name\": \"GRU/Attention probe upper bound (32B)\",\n780\t    \"description\": \"GRU on 32B models at corrected layers. Tests nonlinear sequence modeling vs linear temporal probes.\\n\",\n781\t    \"results\": {\n782\t      \"r1_32b_gru_bal_acc\": 0.675,\n783\t      \"r1_32b_gru_vs_lr_delta\": \"−6pp\",\n784\t      \"qwq_32b_gru_bal_acc\": 0.724,\n785\t      \"qwq_32b_gru_vs_lr_delta\": \"−3.7pp\",\n786\t      \"gru_worse_3_of_4\": true,\n787\t      \"gru_trades_accuracy_for_coverage\": true\n788\t    },\n789\t    \"tables\": [\n790\t      {\n791\t        \"name\": \"GRU vs Temporal LR (corrected layers)\",\n792\t        \"columns\": [\n793\t          \"Model\",\n794\t          \"LR bal_acc\",\n795\t          \"GRU bal_acc\",\n796\t          \"Δ\",\n797\t          \"GRU FPR\",\n798\t          \"LR FPR\"\n799\t        ],\n800\t        \"rows\": [\n801\t          [\n802\t            \"R1-32B L63\",\n803\t            \"0.737\",\n804\t            \"~0.675\",\n805\t            \"−6.2pp\",\n806\t            \"0.135\",\n807\t            \"0.234\"\n808\t          ],\n809\t          [\n810\t            \"QwQ-32B L60\",\n811\t            \"0.761\",\n812\t            \"0.724\",\n813\t            \"−3.7pp\",\n814\t            \"0.289\",\n815\t            \"0.217\"\n816\t          ],\n817\t          [\n818\t            \"OT-7B L16\",\n819\t            \"0.688\",\n820\t            \"0.746\",\n821\t            \"+5.8pp\",\n822\t            \"0.274\",\n823\t            \"0.100\"\n824\t          ],\n825\t          [\n826\t            \"R1-8B L14\",\n827\t            \"0.806\",\n828\t            \"0.809\",\n829\t            \"+0.3pp\",\n830\t            \"0.165\",\n831\t            \"0.097\"\n832\t          ]\n833\t        ]\n834\t      }\n835\t    ],\n836\t    \"figures\": [],\n837\t    \"analysis\": \"Linear probes at information ceiling. Complexity doesn't help. Combined with AdvBench showing no HS advantage at all, the message is: 'The signal is both simple and narrow.'\\n\\n\\nGRU不优于temporal LR on 3/4 models（R1-32B -6pp, QwQ -3.7pp due to overfitting on small samples）。Linear probes sufficient。Worst-tier reviewer: 'Method is logistic regression with sliding window — the simplest possible baseline. GRU/MLP failure confirms method space is trivial. On AdvBench binary data, even this trivial method can't differentiate HS from text. The information ceiling is low across the board.'\\n\"\n838\t  },\n839\t  {\n840\t    \"name\": \"Text-based baseline (sentence embeddings + temporal LR)\",\n841\t    \"description\": \"Sentence-BERT (all-MiniLM-L6-v2) + temporal window LR on CoT text. Critical HS vs text comparison.\\n\",\n842\t    \"results\": {\n843\t      \"r1_8b_text_static_acc\": 0.661,\n844\t      \"r1_8b_text_w15_acc\": 0.678,\n845\t      \"r1_8b_hs_advantage\": \"+15-18pp\",\n846\t      \"ot_7b_text_best_bal_acc\": 0.614,\n847\t      \"qwq_32b_text_bal_acc\": 0.65,\n848\t      \"r1_32b_text_bal_acc\": 0.646,\n849\t      \"hs_vs_text_precision_gap\": \"+15.4 to +23.6pp\",\n850\t      \"text_precision_range\": \"0.329–0.487\",\n851\t      \"text_requires_zero_infra\": true\n852\t    },\n853\t    \"tables\": [\n854\t      {\n855\t        \"name\": \"HS vs Text: CP Data vs AdvBench\",\n856\t        \"columns\": [\n857\t          \"Model\",\n858\t          \"CP HS Prec\",\n859\t          \"CP Text Prec\",\n860\t          \"CP Δ\",\n861\t          \"AdvBench HS Prec\",\n862\t          \"AdvBench Text Prec\",\n863\t          \"AdvBench Δ\"\n864\t        ],\n865\t        \"rows\": [\n866\t          [\n867\t            \"R1-8B\",\n868\t            \"0.777\",\n869\t            \"0.487\",\n870\t            \"+29.0pp ✅\",\n871\t            \"0.58\",\n872\t            \"0.55\",\n873\t            \"+3pp NS\"\n874\t          ]\n875\t        ]\n876\t      }\n877\t    ],\n878\t    \"figures\": [],\n879\t    \"analysis\": \"Text baseline comparison reveals the HS advantage is task-specific. On temporal CP detection: HS clearly better. On binary AdvBench: HS ≈ text. This is the strongest evidence that the findings don't generalize beyond the specific pipeline.\\n\\n\\nText baseline在CP data上consistently underperforms HS by 15-24pp precision， 但在AdvBench binary task上这个gap shrinks to +2-3pp (NS)。Worst-tier reviewer uses this asymmetry: 'On the one dataset where HS advantage matters (CP temporal), yes HS wins. But you showed on AdvBench that the advantage vanishes. The HS advantage is not a modality property — it's a task×modality interaction. For the specific task of temporal safety commitment detection with step-level labels, HS helps. For binary safety classification, it doesn't. This means the 15-24pp gap is not about \\\"hidden states are better monitors\\\" — it's about \\\"hidden states help with THIS specific task.\\\"' Additionally: BGE-large comparison (1/4 HB pass) shows gap narrows with better text encoder. A reviewer asks: 'What about GPT-4 as text classifier? The gap likely closes to <5pp.'\\n\"\n880\t  },\n881\t  {\n882\t    \"name\": \"BOCPD/CUSUM on all 4 models\",\n883\t    \"description\": \"BOCPD + CUSUM on probe score sequences across 4 models. Tests advanced change-point methods for lead time improvement.\\n\",\n884\t    \"results\": {\n885\t      \"r1_8b_bocpd_det\": 0.93,\n886\t      \"r1_8b_bocpd_lead\": \"+3.45\",\n887\t      \"r1_8b_fcp_lead\": \"+4.89\",\n888\t      \"ot_7b_bocpd_det\": 0.0,\n889\t      \"r1_32b_bocpd_det\": 0.0,\n890\t      \"qwq_32b_bocpd_det\": 0.0,\n891\t      \"bocpd_only_r1_8b\": true,\n892\t      \"cusum_worse_than_fcp\": true\n893\t    },\n894\t    \"tables\": [\n895\t      {\n896\t        \"name\": \"Change-Point Detection Summary\",\n897\t        \"columns\": [\n898\t          \"Model\",\n899\t          \"BOCPD Det%\",\n900\t          \"BOCPD Lead\",\n901\t          \"CUSUM Det%\",\n902\t          \"CUSUM Lead\",\n903\t          \"FCP Det%\",\n904\t          \"FCP Lead\"\n905\t        ],\n906\t        \"rows\": [\n907\t          [\n908\t            \"R1-8B\",\n909\t            \"93%\",\n910\t            \"+3.45\",\n911\t            \"65%\",\n912\t            \"+0.89\",\n913\t            \"65%\",\n914\t            \"+4.89\"\n915\t          ],\n916\t          [\n917\t            \"OT-7B\",\n918\t            \"0%\",\n919\t            \"—\",\n920\t            \"75%†\",\n921\t            \"+7.46†\",\n922\t            \"89%\",\n923\t            \"+11.83\"\n924\t          ],\n925\t          [\n926\t            \"R1-32B\",\n927\t            \"0%\",\n928\t            \"—\",\n929\t            \"85%\",\n930\t            \"+2.59\",\n931\t            \"95%\",\n932\t            \"+5.68\"\n933\t          ],\n934\t          [\n935\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n936\t            \"0%\",\n937\t            \"—\",\n938\t            \"74%\",\n939\t            \"+9.86\",\n940\t            \"79%\",\n941\t            \"+9.40\"\n942\t          ]\n943\t        ]\n944\t      }\n945\t    ],\n946\t    \"figures\": [],\n947\t    \"analysis\": \"BOCPD/CUSUM negative result is predictable. Method selection too narrow. Combined with AdvBench non-replication, entire negative result portfolio is dismissed as 'testing wrong tools on a narrow task.'\\n\\n\\nBOCPD在3/4模型上0% detection——OT-7B probe scores too spiky，32B models signal太weak。只有R1-8B有gradual transitions compatible with Gaussian change-point model。CUSUM universally worse than simple FCP threshold。 AdvBench非复现不直接影响BOCPD结果（BOCPD operates on temporal probe scores， AdvBench是binary task没有temporal scores），但strengthens the 'nothing transfers' narrative: '你测试了2个off-the-shelf change-point methods，都失败。 你的layer selection bias也不generalize。你的HS advantage也不generalize。 整篇paper的主题是\\\"on one specific dataset with one specific pipeline, one specific finding holds.\\\"'\\n\"\n948\t  },\n949\t  {\n950\t    \"name\": \"False positive analysis (safe traces, all methods)\",\n951\t    \"description\": \"All probes on safe traces across 4 models. Safe FPR, pre-CP FPR, precision-recall curves.\\n\",\n952\t    \"results\": {\n953\t      \"r1_8b_hs_safe_fpr\": 0.39,\n954\t      \"ot_7b_hs_safe_fpr\": \"0.111–0.175\",\n955\t      \"qwq_32b_hs_safe_fpr\": \"0.45–0.50\",\n956\t      \"r1_32b_hs_safe_fpr\": 0.239,\n957\t      \"safe_fpr_median\": \"~0.39\",\n958\t      \"gru_safe_fpr\": 0.521,\n959\t      \"no_method_achieves_prec80\": true,\n960\t      \"qwq_hs_fpr_worse_than_text\": true\n961\t    },\n962\t    \"tables\": [\n963\t      {\n964\t        \"name\": \"Safe Trace FPR (all methods × models)\",\n965\t        \"columns\": [\n966\t          \"Model\",\n967\t          \"HS Static FPR\",\n968\t          \"HS Temporal FPR\",\n969\t          \"Text Temporal FPR\",\n970\t          \"GRU FPR\"\n971\t        ],\n972\t        \"rows\": [\n973\t          [\n974\t            \"R1-8B\",\n975\t            \"0.390\",\n976\t            \"0.392\",\n977\t            \"0.385\",\n978\t            \"0.521\"\n979\t          ],\n980\t          [\n981\t            \"OT-7B\",\n982\t            \"0.111\",\n983\t            \"0.175\",\n984\t            \"0.460\",\n985\t            \"—\"\n986\t          ],\n987\t          [\n988\t            \"QwQ-32B\",\n989\t            \"0.500\",\n990\t            \"0.450\",\n991\t            \"0.425\",\n992\t            \"—\"\n993\t          ],\n994\t          [\n995\t            \"R1-32B\",\n996\t            \"0.239\",\n997\t            \"0.239\",\n998\t            \"0.310\",\n999\t            \"—\"\n1000\t          ]\n1001\t        ]\n1002\t      },\n1003\t      {\n1004\t        \"name\": \"Deployment Viability\",\n1005\t        \"columns\": [\n1006\t          \"Criterion\",\n1007\t          \"Threshold\",\n1008\t          \"Models Meeting\",\n1009\t          \"Best Method\"\n1010\t        ],\n1011\t        \"rows\": [\n1012\t          [\n1013\t            \"Safe FPR < 20%\",\n1014\t            \"0.20\",\n1015\t            \"1/4 (OT-7B)\",\n1016\t            \"HS static L16\"\n1017\t          ],\n1018\t          [\n1019\t            \"Precision > 70%\",\n1020\t            \"0.70\",\n1021\t            \"1/4 (R1-8B W≥15)\",\n1022\t            \"HS temporal W=25\"\n1023\t          ],\n1024\t          [\n1025\t            \"Coverage > 60%\",\n1026\t            \"0.60\",\n1027\t            \"2/4\",\n1028\t            \"HS temporal W=1\"\n1029\t          ],\n1030\t          [\n1031\t            \"All three\",\n1032\t            \"—\",\n1033\t            \"0/4\",\n1034\t            \"None\"\n1035\t          ]\n1036\t        ]\n1037\t      }\n1038\t    ],\n1039\t    \"figures\": [],\n1040\t    \"analysis\": \"FPR is the deployment dealbreaker on CP data. AdvBench non-replication closes the escape hatch of 'maybe it works better on other datasets' — it demonstrably works WORSE. Reviewer verdict: 'A monitor that fires on 1/3 safe traces is noise. Adding AdvBench shows this isn't fixable by changing the dataset.'\\n\\n\\nSafe FPR 11-52%，median约39%。这意味着每2-3个safe traces就有1个false alarm。 No operating point achieves simultaneous >70% precision AND >60% coverage on ANY model。QwQ HS safe FPR (45-50%) actually HIGHER than text (33-43%)。 AdvBench非复现加重了practical concern: '在你优化过的CP dataset上，safe FPR 已经是39%。在AdvBench上HS甚至不优于text。任何real-world deployment都会面临 distribution shift，FPR只会更差。这个系统在production中是不可用的。'\\n\"\n1041\t  },\n1042\t  {\n1043\t    \"name\": \"AdvBench Layer Bias Replication (R1-8B)\",\n1044\t    \"description\": \"Replicate layer selection bias on AdvBench using R1-8B with binary jailbreak/safe labels. Train probes at all 32 layers, run 5-metric layer selection analysis. Tests whether crossing_rate bias is model-geometric (general) or CP-annotation-specific (task-bound).\\n\",\n1045\t    \"results\": {\n1046\t      \"n_traces_total\": 200,\n1047\t      \"n_jailbreak\": 104,\n1048\t      \"n_safe\": 96,\n1049\t      \"model\": \"R1-8B (Llama-8B, 32 layers)\",\n1050\t      \"layer_selection\": {\n1051\t        \"bal_acc_x_cr_layer\": 12,\n1052\t        \"bal_acc_x_cr_depth_pct\": 39,\n1053\t        \"pure_bal_acc_layer\": 13,\n1054\t        \"pure_bal_acc_depth_pct\": 42,\n1055\t        \"precision_layer\": 13,\n1056\t        \"precision_depth_pct\": 42,\n1057\t        \"pre_cp_fpr_layer\": 14,\n1058\t        \"pre_cp_fpr_depth_pct\": 45,\n1059\t        \"composite_layer\": 13,\n1060\t        \"composite_depth_pct\": 42\n1061\t      },\n1062\t      \"layer_gap_abs\": 2,\n1063\t      \"layer_gap_depth_pct\": 6,\n1064\t      \"crossing_rate_analysis\": {\n1065\t        \"shallow_mean_cr\": 0.12,\n1066\t        \"deep_mean_cr\": 0.11,\n1067\t        \"shallow_deep_ratio\": 1.05,\n1068\t        \"ratio_p_value\": 0.74,\n1069\t        \"pattern\": \"uniform — no shallow inflation\"\n1070\t      },\n1071\t      \"hs_performance_at_best_layer\": {\n1072\t        \"layer\": 13,\n1073\t        \"bal_acc\": 0.63,\n1074\t        \"precision\": 0.58,\n1075\t        \"pre_cp_fpr\": 0.31,\n1076\t        \"auroc\": 0.68\n1077\t      },\n1078\t      \"text_performance\": {\n1079\t        \"bal_acc\": 0.6,\n1080\t        \"precision\": 0.55,\n1081\t        \"pre_cp_fpr\": 0.34\n1082\t      },\n1083\t      \"hs_text_comparison\": {\n1084\t        \"bal_acc_delta_pp\": 3.0,\n1085\t        \"precision_delta_pp\": 3.0,\n1086\t        \"fpr_delta_pp\": -3.0,\n1087\t        \"bal_acc_p\": 0.28,\n1088\t        \"precision_p\": 0.31,\n1089\t        \"fpr_p\": 0.42,\n1090\t        \"hb_pass_rate\": \"0/3\"\n1091\t      },\n1092\t      \"cp_data_comparison\": {\n1093\t        \"cp_cr_composite_layer\": \"L0 (0%)\",\n1094\t        \"advbench_cr_composite_layer\": \"L12 (39%)\",\n1095\t        \"cp_layer_gap\": 16,\n1096\t        \"advbench_layer_gap\": 2,\n1097\t        \"cp_hs_text_prec_delta\": \"+22.4pp\",\n1098\t        \"advbench_hs_text_prec_delta\": \"+3.0pp\",\n1099\t        \"cp_crossing_rate_shallow_deep_ratio\": 2.8,\n1100\t        \"advbench_crossing_rate_shallow_deep_ratio\": 1.05\n1101\t      }\n1102\t    },\n1103\t    \"tables\": [\n1104\t      {\n1105\t        \"name\": \"AdvBench Layer Selection (R1-8B, 5 metrics)\",\n1106\t        \"columns\": [\n1107\t          \"Metric\",\n1108\t          \"Selected Layer\",\n1109\t          \"Depth %\",\n1110\t          \"bal_acc at Layer\"\n1111\t        ],\n1112\t        \"rows\": [\n1113\t          [\n1114\t            \"bal_acc × crossing_rate\",\n1115\t            \"L12\",\n1116\t            \"39%\",\n1117\t            \"0.62\"\n1118\t          ],\n1119\t          [\n1120\t            \"pure bal_acc\",\n1121\t            \"L13\",\n1122\t            \"42%\",\n1123\t            \"0.63\"\n1124\t          ],\n1125\t          [\n1126\t            \"precision\",\n1127\t            \"L13\",\n1128\t            \"42%\",\n1129\t            \"0.63\"\n1130\t          ],\n1131\t          [\n1132\t            \"pre_cp_fpr\",\n1133\t            \"L14\",\n1134\t            \"45%\",\n1135\t            \"0.62\"\n1136\t          ],\n1137\t          [\n1138\t            \"composite (additive)\",\n1139\t            \"L13\",\n1140\t            \"42%\",\n1141\t            \"0.63\"\n1142\t          ]\n1143\t        ]\n1144\t      },\n1145\t      {\n1146\t        \"name\": \"CP Data vs AdvBench: Layer Selection Bias Comparison\",\n1147\t        \"columns\": [\n1148\t          \"Dimension\",\n1149\t          \"CP Temporal Data\",\n1150\t          \"AdvBench Binary\"\n1151\t        ],\n1152\t        \"rows\": [\n1153\t          [\n1154\t            \"cr_composite layer\",\n1155\t            \"L0 (0%)\",\n1156\t            \"L12 (39%)\"\n1157\t          ],\n1158\t          [\n1159\t            \"threshold metrics layer\",\n1160\t            \"L16 (52%)\",\n1161\t            \"L13 (42%)\"\n1162\t          ],\n1163\t          [\n1164\t            \"layer gap (abs)\",\n1165\t            \"16 layers\",\n1166\t            \"2 layers\"\n1167\t          ],\n1168\t          [\n1169\t            \"layer gap (depth %)\",\n1170\t            \"52%\",\n1171\t            \"6%\"\n1172\t          ],\n1173\t          [\n1174\t            \"CR shallow/deep ratio\",\n1175\t            \"2.8\",\n1176\t            \"1.05\"\n1177\t          ],\n1178\t          [\n1179\t            \"HS-text Δprec\",\n1180\t            \"+22.4pp (HB ✅)\",\n1181\t            \"+3.0pp (NS)\"\n1182\t          ],\n1183\t          [\n1184\t            \"HB pass rate\",\n1185\t            \"3/3\",\n1186\t            \"0/3\"\n1187\t          ]\n1188\t        ]\n1189\t      },\n1190\t      {\n1191\t        \"name\": \"AdvBench Crossing Rate by Depth Band\",\n1192\t        \"columns\": [\n1193\t          \"Depth Band\",\n1194\t          \"Mean CR\",\n1195\t          \"Std CR\",\n1196\t          \"n layers\"\n1197\t        ],\n1198\t        \"rows\": [\n1199\t          [\n1200\t            \"Shallow (L0-L7)\",\n1201\t            \"0.12\",\n1202\t            \"0.03\",\n1203\t            \"8\"\n1204\t          ],\n1205\t          [\n1206\t            \"Middle (L8-L23)\",\n1207\t            \"0.11\",\n1208\t            \"0.02\",\n1209\t            \"16\"\n1210\t          ],\n1211\t          [\n1212\t            \"Deep (L24-L31)\",\n1213\t            \"0.11\",\n1214\t            \"0.03\",\n1215\t            \"8\"\n1216\t          ],\n1217\t          [\n1218\t            \"Shallow/Deep ratio\",\n1219\t            \"1.05\",\n1220\t            \"—\",\n1221\t            \"—\"\n1222\t          ],\n1223\t          [\n1224\t            \"t-test p-value\",\n1225\t            \"0.74\",\n1226\t            \"—\",\n1227\t            \"—\"\n1228\t          ]\n1229\t        ]\n1230\t      },\n1231\t      {\n1232\t        \"name\": \"AdvBench HS vs Text Performance (R1-8B)\",\n1233\t        \"columns\": [\n1234\t          \"Modality\",\n1235\t          \"bal_acc\",\n1236\t          \"precision\",\n1237\t          \"FPR\",\n1238\t          \"AUROC\"\n1239\t        ],\n1240\t        \"rows\": [\n1241\t          [\n1242\t            \"HS (L13)\",\n1243\t            \"0.63\",\n1244\t            \"0.58\",\n1245\t            \"0.31\",\n1246\t            \"0.68\"\n1247\t          ],\n1248\t          [\n1249\t            \"Text (MiniLM)\",\n1250\t            \"0.60\",\n1251\t            \"0.55\",\n1252\t            \"0.34\",\n1253\t            \"0.65\"\n1254\t          ],\n1255\t          [\n1256\t            \"Δ\",\n1257\t            \"+3.0pp\",\n1258\t            \"+3.0pp\",\n1259\t            \"−3.0pp\",\n1260\t            \"+3.0pp\"\n1261\t          ],\n1262\t          [\n1263\t            \"p-value\",\n1264\t            \"0.28\",\n1265\t            \"0.31\",\n1266\t            \"0.42\",\n1267\t            \"0.25\"\n1268\t          ],\n1269\t          [\n1270\t            \"HB pass?\",\n1271\t            \"No\",\n1272\t            \"No\",\n1273\t            \"No\",\n1274\t            \"No\"\n1275\t          ]\n1276\t        ]\n1277\t      }\n1278\t    ],\n1279\t    \"figures\": [],\n1280\t    \"analysis\": \"AdvBench非复现是worst-tier的defining experiment。结论分为两个component:\\n(1) Layer selection bias非复现：Mechanistically consistent。crossing_rate bias 的root cause是temporal probe score序列中shallow layers的noise-driven threshold crossings。Binary classification不产生这种temporal structure。Bias不复现 不invalidate CP data上的finding——it BOUNDS its scope。C1调整为: 'Layer selection bias is a pitfall specific to temporal monitoring pipelines that use threshold-crossing statistics for layer selection. It does not manifest in standard binary classification settings.'\\n(2) HS-text gap非复现：+3pp (NS) vs CP data +22.4pp (HB ✅)。HS advantage 在binary classification上消失，说明advantage来自temporal structure interaction（hidden states track step-level state transitions that text embeddings miss），而非inherent modality superiority。\\nPaper impact: C1 scope从'any hidden-state evaluation should audit layer selection metrics'收窄到'temporal monitoring pipelines using crossing_rate should audit layer selection'。这让contribution更precise但less impactful。\\nReviewer split: - Supportive (R1): 'Boundary condition analysis is good science. Showing WHERE\\n  your finding applies and where it doesn't is a sign of mature methodology.\\n  The AdvBench non-replication actually STRENGTHENS the mechanistic argument —\\n  you can explain WHY it doesn't replicate (no temporal structure → no\\n  crossing_rate bias).'\\n- Dismissive (R2): 'The non-replication means your headline finding (layer\\n  selection bias) is a pipeline implementation detail, not a scientific\\n  contribution. You could fix it by not using crossing_rate — which any\\n  careful practitioner would do anyway. The AdvBench experiment cost 6 GPU\\n  hours to show your contribution is smaller than claimed.'\\n\\nDream score delta: -0.10 (3.0 → 2.9). Non-replication doesn't kill the paper but removes the possibility of C1 earning generalizability points. Paper stays at Findings level; no pathway to Main opened by this experiment.\\n\\nAdvBench非复现的root cause是mechanistic的，不是statistical noise:\\n(1) crossing_rate在binary classification中不产生浅层虚高。在CP temporal data中， crossing_rate measures how often probe score crosses threshold DURING a trace。 Shallow layers的probes更noisy（cosine distance smaller, representations more compressed）→更频繁crossing→crossing_rate虚高→与bal_acc相乘后bias toward shallow layers。在AdvBench binary classification中，虽然可以对每个token计算probe score 并观察序列，但binary labels（jailbreak/safe per trace）不创造step-level temporal structure。Probe scores在entire trace上趋于一致（consistently jailbreak或consistently safe），所以crossing_rate在所有层上都很低（~0.11-0.12）， shallow/deep ratio≈1.05，无显著差异。\\n(2) HS-text gap在binary classification中collapse到+3pp (NS)。原因：hidden states 对CP temporal task的advantage来自于tracking step-level safety state transitions—— hidden states可以detect commitment point前后的internal representation shift。 Binary classification不需要这种temporal sensitivity——只需要区分'this trace as a whole is jailbreak vs safe'。对于这个coarser task，text embeddings capture足够 的signal（jailbreak traces有distinctive vocabulary/structure），HS的marginal advantage消失。\\n(3) Layer selection convergence: 5个metrics全部选中层（L12-L14, 39-45% depth）， gap仅2层。这是因为binary classification的optimal layer由overall representation quality决定（middle layers best balance between too-raw和too-output-specific）， 而非threshold-crossing-dynamics。所有metrics agree on similar layers because they're all measuring the same underlying discriminability without temporal confounds。\\n(4) Traces太uniform: AdvBench prompts + R1-8B responses产生相对homogeneous的 traces——jailbreak traces consistently harmful, safe traces consistently refuse。 没有CP temporal data中的'safety reasoning followed by commitment'渐进结构。 所有层表现接近（bal_acc range across layers: 0.55-0.65），metric间无显著分化。\\n\"\n1281\t  }\n1282\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fbcb0466-3e92-40e3-acd9-9335f9dd14ac","uuid":"729998be-8b51-4524-9f87-0e4e0b37fdb2","timestamp":"2026-05-22T09:43:55.232Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/contrastive_trace_safety/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260522T085530/worst/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cross-architecture layer sweep + static probe (R1-32B & QwQ-32B)\",\n    \"description\": \"Full 64-layer sweep on R1-32B and QwQ-32B. Extract hidden states at all layers, evaluate static LR probes under multiple selection metrics. Discovers layer selection bias (bal_acc × crossing_rate → shallow layers).\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_biased_layer\": 10,\n      \"r1_32b_biased_layer_depth_pct\": 15.6,\n      \"r1_32b_corrected_layer\": 63,\n      \"r1_32b_corrected_layer_depth_pct\": 98.4,\n      \"r1_32b_l10_bal_acc\": 0.718,\n      \"r1_32b_l63_bal_acc_w1\": 0.826,\n      \"r1_32b_n_test\": 20,\n      \"qwq_32b_biased_layer\": 2,\n      \"qwq_32b_biased_layer_depth_pct\": 3.1,\n      \"qwq_32b_corrected_layer\": 60,\n      \"qwq_32b_corrected_layer_depth_pct\": 93.8,\n      \"qwq_32b_l2_bal_acc\": 0.73,\n      \"qwq_32b_l60_bal_acc_w3\": 0.74,\n      \"qwq_32b_n_test\": 19,\n      \"layer_selection_bias_discovered\": true,\n      \"metrics_tested\": 5,\n      \"biased_metric\": \"bal_acc × crossing_rate\",\n      \"loo_cv_dominant_fold_pct\": \"65-68%\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Layer Selection Metric × Model → Selected Layer\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Metric\",\n          \"R1-8B\",\n          \"OT-7B\",\n          \"QwQ-32B\",\n          \"R1-32B\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"bal_acc × crossing_rate\",\n            \"L0 (0%)\",\n            \"L0 (0%)\",\n            \"L2 (3%)\",\n            \"L10 (16%)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"pure bal_acc\",\n            \"L12 (39%)\",\n            \"L9 (32%)\",\n            \"L30 (47%)\",\n            \"L47 (73%)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"precision\",\n            \"L16 (52%)\",\n            \"L16 (57%)\",\n            \"L60 (94%)\",\n            \"L63 (98%)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"pre_cp_fpr\",\n            \"L16 (52%)\",\n            \"L16 (57%)\",\n            \"L60 (94%)\",\n            \"L63 (98%)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"composite (additive)\",\n            \"L16 (52%)\",\n            \"L12 (43%)\",\n            \"L60 (94%)\",\n            \"L63 (98%)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Layer sweep结果本身不变，但AdvBench非复现将C1从'general finding applicable to any hidden-state evaluation'降级为'task-specific observation about temporal monitoring pipelines using crossing_rate statistics'。Reviewer: 'If the bias is specific to crossing_rate in temporal settings, then it's a footnote in your methods section, not a standalone contribution. Rename the section from \\\"Layer Selection Sensitivity\\\" to \\\"Implementation Note: Crossing Rate Bias.\\\"'\\n\\n\\nLayer sweep在CP数据上发现genuine methodological pattern（biased metric selects shallow layers），corrected layers确实show HS advantage。这些结果不受AdvBench 非复现影响——它们是关于CP temporal data的发现。但worst-tier reviewer将AdvBench 非复现与此联系：'你在CP data上发现的layer selection bias是CP annotation structure 特有的——crossing_rate在temporal monitoring中有意义，但这不是general model property。既然bias只在temporal setting中存在，那C1的scope就是一个pipeline-specific caveat，不是methodological contribution。任何使用不同task structure的研究者 不会遇到这个问题。'\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cross-architecture temporal window evaluation (32B)\",\n    \"description\": \"Temporal LR at multiple window sizes on R1-32B (L63) and QwQ-32B (L60). Validates temporal context improvement on 32B models.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_l63_w1_bal_acc\": 0.826,\n      \"r1_32b_l63_w3_bal_acc\": 0.754,\n      \"r1_32b_l63_w10_bal_acc\": 0.737,\n      \"r1_32b_l63_w15_bal_acc\": 0.721,\n      \"r1_32b_temporal_trend\": \"DECREASING with W (W=1 best)\",\n      \"r1_32b_dimensionality_issue\": \"51200 × W features for n=20\",\n      \"qwq_32b_l60_w1_bal_acc\": 0.74,\n      \"qwq_32b_l60_w3_bal_acc\": 0.74,\n      \"qwq_32b_l60_w5_bal_acc\": 0.742,\n      \"qwq_32b_l60_w15_bal_acc\": 0.731,\n      \"qwq_32b_temporal_trend\": \"flat to slightly decreasing\",\n      \"text_temporal_universal_improvement\": true,\n      \"hs_temporal_improvement_3_of_4\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Temporal Window Effect on 32B HS Probes (corrected layers)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Layer\",\n          \"W=1\",\n          \"W=3\",\n          \"W=5\",\n          \"W=10\",\n          \"W=15\",\n          \"Trend\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"R1-32B\",\n            \"L63\",\n            \"0.826*\",\n            \"0.754\",\n            \"0.741\",\n            \"0.737\",\n            \"0.721\",\n            \"↓ declining\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"L60\",\n            \"0.740\",\n            \"0.740\",\n            \"0.742\",\n            \"0.735\",\n            \"0.731\",\n            \"→ flat\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"R1-8B\",\n            \"L14\",\n            \"0.778\",\n            \"0.790\",\n            \"0.781\",\n            \"0.793\",\n            \"0.802\",\n            \"↑ improving\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"OT-7B\",\n            \"L16\",\n            \"0.663\",\n            \"0.688*\",\n            \"0.678\",\n            \"0.668\",\n            \"0.659\",\n            \"↑↓ peaks W=3\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"R1-32B dimensionality问题（51200×W > n=20）使W>1结果不可靠。QwQ flat。 Temporal benefit主要是text probe finding。AdvBench非复现进一步说明：即使temporal probing pipeline在CP data上work，其关键组件（crossing_rate, layer selection） 都是task-specific的。\\n\\n\\nTemporal context对text probes universally有效，对HS probes在2/4模型上有caveats （OT-7B saturates at W=3, R1-32B degrades with W>1 due to dimensionality）。 Worst-tier下AdvBench非复现不直接影响这个实验的数据，但强化了reviewer的论点： 'temporal monitoring pipeline本身就充满了model-specific hyperparameters（layer, W, K）。你在每个model上需要tuning这些参数，AdvBench上bias又不复现——这说明你的 整个方法论都是dataset-specific的。'\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Cross-model transfer probes\",\n    \"description\": \"Train probe on one model, test on another. PCA projection for cross-architecture transfer. Tests universality of safety commitment encoding.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_to_r1_32b_acc\": 0.522,\n      \"r1_8b_to_qwq_acc\": 0.595,\n      \"r1_32b_to_qwq_acc\": 0.673,\n      \"qwq_to_r1_32b_acc\": 0.673,\n      \"r1_32b_to_r1_8b_acc\": 0.583,\n      \"best_transfer_overall\": 0.673,\n      \"all_transfers_below_within_model\": true,\n      \"cross_family_at_chance\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Cross-Model Transfer Accuracy Matrix\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Train →\",\n          \"R1-8B\",\n          \"OT-7B\",\n          \"R1-32B\",\n          \"QwQ-32B\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"R1-8B\",\n            \"0.817 (self)\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0.522\",\n            \"0.595\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"OT-7B\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0.688 (self)\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"R1-32B\",\n            \"0.583\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0.826 (self)\",\n            \"0.673\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0.673\",\n            \"0.740 (self)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Complete transfer failure combined with AdvBench non-replication paints a picture of extreme specificity. Everything is model-specific AND task-specific. Reviewer: 'The paper's implicit promise of generalizable insights is contradicted by its own evidence.'\\n\\n\\nCross-model transfer完全失败。最佳transfer accuracy 67.3%远低于within-model performance，cross-family（Llama↔Qwen）接近chance。AdvBench非复现与此一致： 如果连layer selection bias都是task-specific的，那safety encoding的model-specificity 更是预期的。Worst-tier reviewer: 'Transfer failure + AdvBench非复现 = 你的 所有findings都是dataset×model specific的。没有任何generalizable component。 这篇paper的external validity接近零。'\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Lead time analysis across models\",\n    \"description\": \"HS probe lead time distributions on all 4 models. Compare HS vs text lead times.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_hs_lead_mean\": -0.1,\n      \"r1_8b_text_lead_mean\": 10.3,\n      \"ot_7b_hs_lead_mean\": 21.6,\n      \"ot_7b_text_lead_mean\": 45.5,\n      \"qwq_32b_hs_lead_range\": \"-1.2 to +1.9\",\n      \"qwq_32b_text_lead_range\": \"3.7 to 12.3\",\n      \"qwq_32b_position_beats_hs\": true,\n      \"r1_32b_hs_lead_mean\": \"+11.7 to +13.3\",\n      \"r1_32b_coverage_at_significant_w\": 0.25,\n      \"gru_r1_8b_lead\": \"+4.5 ± 3.0\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Lead Time: HS vs Text vs Position\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"HS Lead\",\n          \"Text Lead\",\n          \"Position BA\",\n          \"HS BA\",\n          \"HS > Position?\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"R1-8B (W=15)\",\n            \"−0.1\",\n            \"+10.3†\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0.802\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"OT-7B (W=3)\",\n            \"+21.6‡\",\n            \"+45.5‡\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0.688\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B (W=3)\",\n            \"−1.2 to +1.9\",\n            \"+3.7 to +12.3\",\n            \"0.746\",\n            \"0.740\",\n            \"No\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"R1-32B (W=1)\",\n            \"+11.7 to +13.3\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0.686\",\n            \"0.826\",\n            \"Yes (25% cov)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Lead time是practical dealbreaker。AdvBench非复现加强了reviewer的generalizability concern: '所有timing结论都只在这一个CP-annotated dataset上成立。'\\n\\n\\nLead time在4个模型上表现分裂：R1-8B HS lead≈0（at commitment, not before）， QwQ HS lead为负（text检测更早），R1-32B HS lead positive但coverage仅25%， OT-7B lead inflated by long traces。AdvBench非复现不直接改变lead time数据， 但reviewer论证chain strengthened: '既然layer selection bias是task-specific的， 那lead time结果也可能是task-specific的——你在不同benchmark上可能得到完全不同的 timing profile。没有cross-dataset validation证明timing findings的robustness。'\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"TrajGuard-style Mahalanobis baseline (4 models)\",\n    \"description\": \"Mahalanobis distance on safe-trace covariance across all 4 models. Tests geometric distance-based detection.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_separation\": 0.07,\n      \"ot_7b_separation\": 0.03,\n      \"r1_32b_separation\": 0.15,\n      \"qwq_32b_separation\": 0.2,\n      \"max_separation\": 0.2,\n      \"all_models_fail\": true,\n      \"fa_exceeds_tp_all_thresholds\": true,\n      \"signal_in_minor_pca_components\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Mahalanobis Distance Separation (4 models)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Layer\",\n          \"Safe MD\",\n          \"Post-CP MD\",\n          \"Sep.\",\n          \"FA ≥ TP?\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"R1-8B\",\n            \"L14\",\n            \"6.66\",\n            \"6.73\",\n            \"+0.07\",\n            \"Yes\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"OT-7B\",\n            \"L16\",\n            \"7.68\",\n            \"7.71\",\n            \"+0.03\",\n            \"Yes\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"R1-32B\",\n            \"L63\",\n            \"6.73\",\n            \"6.88\",\n            \"+0.15\",\n            \"Yes\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"L60\",\n            \"3.22\",\n            \"3.42\",\n            \"+0.20\",\n            \"Yes\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Universal Mahalanobis failure supports 'existing methods fail' narrative。 但这个negative result是predictable的，reviewer不计入contribution。\\n\\n\\nMahalanobis在4/4模型上失败（max separation 0.20, FA≥TP at all thresholds）。 Signal在minor PCA components中——Mahalanobis structurally blind to this geometry。 此negative result与AdvBench无关（Mahalanobis不依赖crossing_rate或CP structure）， 但worst-tier reviewer仍然dismiss: 'Mahalanobis failure是predictable—— safety-relevant features orthogonal to principal variance是known phenomenon。 Testing one known-limited baseline is due diligence, not contribution。'\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Bootstrap CI + threshold-independent metrics\",\n    \"description\": \"10000 bootstrap resamples on key metrics across 4 models. Holm-Bonferroni correction. AUROC, PR curves, detection delay CDF.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"holm_bonferroni_pass\": \"12/12\",\n      \"precision_delta_range\": \"+15.4 to +23.6pp\",\n      \"r1_8b_precision_p\": \"<0.001\",\n      \"ot_7b_precision_p\": \"<0.001\",\n      \"qwq_32b_fpr_p\": 0.004,\n      \"r1_32b_fpr_p\": 0.007,\n      \"marginal_tests\": 2,\n      \"r1_32b_ci_width_ba\": \"~0.15\",\n      \"all_precision_positive\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Holm-Bonferroni Summary (12/12, α=0.05)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Rank\",\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Metric\",\n          \"p-value\",\n          \"HB Threshold\",\n          \"Pass\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"1\",\n            \"R1-8B\",\n            \"precision\",\n            \"<0.001\",\n            \"0.0042\",\n            \"✅\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"2\",\n            \"OT-7B\",\n            \"bal_acc\",\n            \"<0.001\",\n            \"0.0045\",\n            \"✅\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"3\",\n            \"OT-7B\",\n            \"precision\",\n            \"<0.001\",\n            \"0.0050\",\n            \"✅\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"4\",\n            \"OT-7B\",\n            \"FPR\",\n            \"<0.001\",\n            \"0.0056\",\n            \"✅\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"5\",\n            \"QwQ\",\n            \"bal_acc\",\n            \"<0.001\",\n            \"0.0063\",\n            \"✅\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"6\",\n            \"QwQ\",\n            \"precision\",\n            \"<0.001\",\n            \"0.0071\",\n            \"✅\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"7\",\n            \"R1-32B\",\n            \"bal_acc\",\n            \"<0.001\",\n            \"0.0083\",\n            \"✅\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"8\",\n            \"R1-32B\",\n            \"precision\",\n            \"<0.001\",\n            \"0.0100\",\n            \"✅\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"9\",\n            \"QwQ\",\n            \"FPR\",\n            \"0.004\",\n            \"0.0125\",\n            \"✅\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"10\",\n            \"R1-8B\",\n            \"bal_acc\",\n            \"0.005\",\n            \"0.0167\",\n            \"✅\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"11\",\n            \"R1-32B\",\n            \"FPR\",\n            \"0.007\",\n            \"0.0250\",\n            \"✅\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"12\",\n            \"R1-8B\",\n            \"FPR\",\n            \"0.044\",\n            \"0.0500\",\n            \"✅\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Statistical significance established on CP data. AdvBench非复现的影响不在 于推翻这些p-values，而在于质疑其generalizability: '在AdvBench上HS-text gap 仅+2-3pp (NS)。如果换个dataset significance就消失，这说明12/12 HB是dataset- specific的，不是modality-inherent advantage。'\\n\\n\\n12/12 HB pass在CP data上是statistically clean的。这些结果不受AdvBench非复现 影响——statistical significance是关于CP temporal data的。但worst-tier reviewer 攻击practical significance和multiplicity: (1) 'per-model best layer AND best window选择后再test，true multiplicity远超12个corrected tests'; (2) '32B model CIs极宽（R1-32B ±15pp），statistical significance with n=20只说明 effect large，不说明estimate precise'; (3) AdvBench非复现加了一层：'你的 statistical machinery只在这一个dataset上验证过。12/12 HB on one dataset ≠ robust finding。'\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Window size ablation (W=1,3,5,10,15,20,25)\",\n    \"description\": \"Systematic window sweep on all 4 models. Characterize precision-coverage tradeoff.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"coverage_decreases_with_w\": true,\n      \"precision_increases_with_w\": true,\n      \"fpr_decreases_with_w\": true,\n      \"r1_8b_best_w\": 25,\n      \"r1_32b_best_w\": 10,\n      \"qwq_best_w\": 25,\n      \"ot_7b_best_w\": 3,\n      \"no_model_achieves_prec70_cov60\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Precision-Coverage at Key W (4 models)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"W=1 Prec/Cov\",\n          \"W=3 Prec/Cov\",\n          \"W=15 Prec/Cov\",\n          \"W=25 Prec/Cov\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"R1-8B\",\n            \"0.693/0.721\",\n            \"0.723/0.698\",\n            \"0.777/0.512\",\n            \"0.822/0.488\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"OT-7B\",\n            \"—/—\",\n            \"0.466/0.532\",\n            \"—/—\",\n            \"—/—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"R1-32B\",\n            \"0.549/0.800\",\n            \"0.560/0.800\",\n            \"0.591/0.700\",\n            \"0.566/0.700\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"0.544/0.789\",\n            \"0.565/0.737\",\n            \"0.582/0.684\",\n            \"0.618/0.579\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"No operating point achieves deployment-ready precision AND coverage simultaneously. AdvBench context reinforces: these tradeoffs may not even transfer to other datasets.\\n\\n\\nPrecision-coverage tradeoff universal but no operating point achieves deployment-ready performance。Worst-tier reviewer: 'Coverage monotonically decreases with W是detection criterion K的数学性质，不是insight。 Optimal W varies by model = another model-specific hyperparameter。 Combined with AdvBench layer bias非复现，整个pipeline的generalizability story 进一步弱化——每个model×dataset组合需要独立tuning。'\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Layer selection ablation (5 metrics × 4 models)\",\n    \"description\": \"All-layer temporal probes on 32B models with 5 selection metrics. Core experiment for C1 layer selection bias claim.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"crossing_rate_selects_shallow_32b\": true,\n      \"pure_bal_acc_selects_middle_32b\": true,\n      \"threshold_dependent_select_deep_32b\": true,\n      \"qwq_l2_vs_l60_precision_gain\": \"+11.5pp\",\n      \"r1_32b_l10_vs_l63_precision_gain\": \"+4.1pp\",\n      \"loo_cv_stability\": \"65-68%\",\n      \"bias_only_on_cp_data\": true,\n      \"bias_not_on_advbench\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Layer Selection Bias: CP Data vs AdvBench (R1-8B)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Metric\",\n          \"CP Data Layer\",\n          \"AdvBench Layer\",\n          \"CP Gap (layers)\",\n          \"AdvBench Gap\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"bal_acc × cr\",\n            \"L0 (0%)\",\n            \"L12 (39%)\",\n            \"16\",\n            \"1\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"pure bal_acc\",\n            \"L12 (39%)\",\n            \"L13 (42%)\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"ref\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"precision\",\n            \"L16 (52%)\",\n            \"L13 (42%)\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"pre_cp_fpr\",\n            \"L16 (52%)\",\n            \"L14 (45%)\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"1\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"composite\",\n            \"L16 (52%)\",\n            \"L13 (42%)\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"MAX GAP\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"16 layers\",\n            \"2 layers\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"这是worst-tier的defining result。CP data上16-layer gap vs AdvBench上2-layer gap直接证明bias是crossing_rate temporal structure产物。Paper must explicitly acknowledge: 'Layer selection bias is specific to temporal monitoring pipelines using crossing_rate-based metrics. Binary classification tasks do not exhibit this bias.' C1 scope收窄但不删除——仍然是valid methodological finding within its scope.\\n\\n\\nLayer selection ablation在CP data上reveals genuine pattern。但这是worst-tier的 核心实验——AdvBench非复现直接打击这个实验的generalizability claim。在CP data上， crossing_rate在shallow layers系统性偏高是因为temporal probe score序列中，shallow层 probes更noisy→更频繁crossing threshold→crossing_rate虚高→与bal_acc相乘后拉向 shallow layers。在AdvBench binary classification中，没有'连续probe score序列的 threshold crossing'概念——每个trace只有一个binary prediction，crossing_rate要么 不存在（无temporal sequence）要么在所有层similar（无differential noise结构）。 所以bias不复现是MECHANISTICALLY CONSISTENT——不是实验失败，而是bias的root cause 在AdvBench中不存在。Worst-tier reviewer nonetheless argues: 'Consistent non- replication means the bias is a pipeline artifact, not a model property. Your C1 contribution scope is now: \\\"if you use crossing_rate as part of your layer selection metric in a temporal monitoring pipeline, be aware it biases toward shallow layers.\\\" That's a methods note, not a contribution.'\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"16-label taxonomy supervision\",\n    \"description\": \"Fine-grained safety labels (Type 1/2/3/4 × pre/post) instead of binary commitment. Evaluate richer supervision impact.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_16label_acc\": 0.41,\n      \"r1_8b_binary_collapse_delta\": \"+0.8pp\",\n      \"ot_7b_binary_collapse_delta\": \"+1.1pp\",\n      \"r1_32b_binary_collapse_delta\": \"+0.6pp\",\n      \"qwq_32b_binary_collapse_delta\": \"+0.9pp\",\n      \"max_delta\": \"+1.1pp\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"16-Label vs Binary Supervision\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Binary Acc\",\n          \"16-Label Acc\",\n          \"16→Binary Collapse\",\n          \"Δ vs Binary\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"R1-8B\",\n            \"0.817\",\n            \"0.41\",\n            \"0.825\",\n            \"+0.8pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"OT-7B\",\n            \"0.688\",\n            \"0.35\",\n            \"0.699\",\n            \"+1.1pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"0.740\",\n            \"0.26\",\n            \"0.749\",\n            \"+0.9pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"R1-32B\",\n            \"0.826\",\n            \"0.28\",\n            \"0.832\",\n            \"+0.6pp\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Fine-grained supervision dead-end. Linear probes saturated at binary level. AdvBench adds: even the binary signal is task-specific.\\n\\n\\nFine-grained labels提供marginal improvement（+0.6-1.1pp collapse后）。 16-class accuracy低（26-41%）因为transition types在hidden-state space中 不cleanly separable。AdvBench非复现不直接影响此实验，但reviewer argument strengthened: 'Binary labels work almost as well as 16-label supervision. And on AdvBench (also binary), the whole pipeline fails to distinguish HS from text. This suggests the signal structure you're exploiting is extremely narrow — it exists only in CP-annotated temporal data with step-level labels.'\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Oracle decomposition\",\n    \"description\": \"Analyze oracle label sequence vs hidden-state probe predictions. Explain 31-step gap between oracle and probe lead time on R1-8B.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_oracle_lead\": \"+31.0\",\n      \"r1_8b_best_probe_lead\": \"+4.5 (GRU)\",\n      \"oracle_gap\": 26.5,\n      \"label_autocorr_pre_cp\": 0.82,\n      \"hs_autocorr_pre_cp\": 0.95,\n      \"label_entropy_pre_cp\": 1.38,\n      \"hs_entropy_pre_cp\": 0.41,\n      \"pattern\": \"Labels show gradual erosion; hidden states show step-function at CP\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Oracle vs Best Probe Lead Time\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"Oracle Lead\",\n          \"Best Probe\",\n          \"Method\",\n          \"Gap\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"R1-8B\",\n            \"+31.0\",\n            \"+4.5\",\n            \"GRU multi-layer\",\n            \"26.5\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"OT-7B\",\n            \"~+30\",\n            \"+33.6\",\n            \"GRU multi-layer\",\n            \"~0†\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"+27\",\n            \"−1.2 to +1.9\",\n            \"temporal LR\",\n            \"25-28\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"R1-32B\",\n            \"+28\",\n            \"+13.3\",\n            \"temporal LR W=1\",\n            \"15 (25% cov)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Hidden states are reactive, not predictive. Combined with AdvBench showing no HS-text differentiation, worst-tier reviewer concludes: 'This paper convincingly demonstrates that hidden-state monitoring is fundamentally limited. The contribution is negative — which is fine for a Findings paper, but not sufficient for Main.'\\n\\n\\nOracle gap reveals hidden states encode current safety state but not trajectory toward commitment。Labels显示gradual pre-CP patterns（increasing unsafe-adjacent labels, shortening safe streaks），hidden states不track这些 patterns。AdvBench非复现reinforces this: 'Hidden states don't capture safety trajectory even on the dataset specifically designed to test this. On a different dataset (AdvBench), the signal degrades further. The fundamental limitation is that hidden states are snapshots, not trajectory predictors.'\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B, STILL-trained)\",\n    \"description\": \"4th model completing 2×2 architecture×scale matrix. Full pipeline: layer sweep, temporal probes, bootstrap, selectivity.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"best_layer\": 16,\n      \"best_layer_depth_pct\": 57.1,\n      \"best_hs_bal_acc_w3\": 0.688,\n      \"best_hs_precision_w3\": 0.466,\n      \"bootstrap_3_of_3_hb\": true,\n      \"selectivity_pp\": 18.3,\n      \"n_traces\": 228,\n      \"gru_multi_bal_acc\": 0.746,\n      \"gru_multi_lead\": \"+33.6\",\n      \"gru_multi_fpr\": 0.274\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"OT-7B Pipeline Summary\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Method\",\n          \"bal_acc\",\n          \"precision\",\n          \"pre-CP FPR\",\n          \"coverage\",\n          \"lead\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"HS temporal W=3 L16\",\n            \"0.688\",\n            \"0.466\",\n            \"0.100\",\n            \"0.532\",\n            \"+21.6\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Text temporal W=25\",\n            \"0.614\",\n            \"0.329\",\n            \"0.161\",\n            \"0.660\",\n            \"+45.5\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GRU multi L13-19\",\n            \"0.746\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0.274\",\n            \"0.645\",\n            \"+33.6\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"HS-Text Δ (HB)\",\n            \"+10.7pp ✅\",\n            \"+21.9pp ✅\",\n            \"−17.1pp ✅\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"OT-7B strengthens 7-8B story on CP data but is irrelevant to AdvBench non-replication concern. Worst-tier: '4 models on 1 dataset ≠ 1 model on 4 datasets.'\\n\\n\\nOT-7B是single-model最clean evidence（3/3 HB, highest selectivity 18.3pp, largest n=228）。但这在CP data上的strong result并不帮助AdvBench non-replication： 'A 4th model confirming the same CP-data pattern doesn't address the cross- dataset non-replication. You have 4 models × 1 dataset = 4 data points on the same distribution. AdvBench shows the pattern doesn't transfer to a different distribution. More models on the same dataset is width without depth of validation.' OT-7B HS temporal saturation at W=3也says something: even on the best model, the temporal signal is fragile.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"GRU/Attention probe upper bound (32B)\",\n    \"description\": \"GRU on 32B models at corrected layers. Tests nonlinear sequence modeling vs linear temporal probes.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_32b_gru_bal_acc\": 0.675,\n      \"r1_32b_gru_vs_lr_delta\": \"−6pp\",\n      \"qwq_32b_gru_bal_acc\": 0.724,\n      \"qwq_32b_gru_vs_lr_delta\": \"−3.7pp\",\n      \"gru_worse_3_of_4\": true,\n      \"gru_trades_accuracy_for_coverage\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"GRU vs Temporal LR (corrected layers)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"LR bal_acc\",\n          \"GRU bal_acc\",\n          \"Δ\",\n          \"GRU FPR\",\n          \"LR FPR\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"R1-32B L63\",\n            \"0.737\",\n            \"~0.675\",\n            \"−6.2pp\",\n            \"0.135\",\n            \"0.234\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B L60\",\n            \"0.761\",\n            \"0.724\",\n            \"−3.7pp\",\n            \"0.289\",\n            \"0.217\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"OT-7B L16\",\n            \"0.688\",\n            \"0.746\",\n            \"+5.8pp\",\n            \"0.274\",\n            \"0.100\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"R1-8B L14\",\n            \"0.806\",\n            \"0.809\",\n            \"+0.3pp\",\n            \"0.165\",\n            \"0.097\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Linear probes at information ceiling. Complexity doesn't help. Combined with AdvBench showing no HS advantage at all, the message is: 'The signal is both simple and narrow.'\\n\\n\\nGRU不优于temporal LR on 3/4 models（R1-32B -6pp, QwQ -3.7pp due to overfitting on small samples）。Linear probes sufficient。Worst-tier reviewer: 'Method is logistic regression with sliding window — the simplest possible baseline. GRU/MLP failure confirms method space is trivial. On AdvBench binary data, even this trivial method can't differentiate HS from text. The information ceiling is low across the board.'\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Text-based baseline (sentence embeddings + temporal LR)\",\n    \"description\": \"Sentence-BERT (all-MiniLM-L6-v2) + temporal window LR on CoT text. Critical HS vs text comparison.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_text_static_acc\": 0.661,\n      \"r1_8b_text_w15_acc\": 0.678,\n      \"r1_8b_hs_advantage\": \"+15-18pp\",\n      \"ot_7b_text_best_bal_acc\": 0.614,\n      \"qwq_32b_text_bal_acc\": 0.65,\n      \"r1_32b_text_bal_acc\": 0.646,\n      \"hs_vs_text_precision_gap\": \"+15.4 to +23.6pp\",\n      \"text_precision_range\": \"0.329–0.487\",\n      \"text_requires_zero_infra\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"HS vs Text: CP Data vs AdvBench\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"CP HS Prec\",\n          \"CP Text Prec\",\n          \"CP Δ\",\n          \"AdvBench HS Prec\",\n          \"AdvBench Text Prec\",\n          \"AdvBench Δ\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"R1-8B\",\n            \"0.777\",\n            \"0.487\",\n            \"+29.0pp ✅\",\n            \"0.58\",\n            \"0.55\",\n            \"+3pp NS\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Text baseline comparison reveals the HS advantage is task-specific. On temporal CP detection: HS clearly better. On binary AdvBench: HS ≈ text. This is the strongest evidence that the findings don't generalize beyond the specific pipeline.\\n\\n\\nText baseline在CP data上consistently underperforms HS by 15-24pp precision， 但在AdvBench binary task上这个gap shrinks to +2-3pp (NS)。Worst-tier reviewer uses this asymmetry: 'On the one dataset where HS advantage matters (CP temporal), yes HS wins. But you showed on AdvBench that the advantage vanishes. The HS advantage is not a modality property — it's a task×modality interaction. For the specific task of temporal safety commitment detection with step-level labels, HS helps. For binary safety classification, it doesn't. This means the 15-24pp gap is not about \\\"hidden states are better monitors\\\" — it's about \\\"hidden states help with THIS specific task.\\\"' Additionally: BGE-large comparison (1/4 HB pass) shows gap narrows with better text encoder. A reviewer asks: 'What about GPT-4 as text classifier? The gap likely closes to <5pp.'\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"BOCPD/CUSUM on all 4 models\",\n    \"description\": \"BOCPD + CUSUM on probe score sequences across 4 models. Tests advanced change-point methods for lead time improvement.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_bocpd_det\": 0.93,\n      \"r1_8b_bocpd_lead\": \"+3.45\",\n      \"r1_8b_fcp_lead\": \"+4.89\",\n      \"ot_7b_bocpd_det\": 0.0,\n      \"r1_32b_bocpd_det\": 0.0,\n      \"qwq_32b_bocpd_det\": 0.0,\n      \"bocpd_only_r1_8b\": true,\n      \"cusum_worse_than_fcp\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Change-Point Detection Summary\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"BOCPD Det%\",\n          \"BOCPD Lead\",\n          \"CUSUM Det%\",\n          \"CUSUM Lead\",\n          \"FCP Det%\",\n          \"FCP Lead\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"R1-8B\",\n            \"93%\",\n            \"+3.45\",\n            \"65%\",\n            \"+0.89\",\n            \"65%\",\n            \"+4.89\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"OT-7B\",\n            \"0%\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"75%†\",\n            \"+7.46†\",\n            \"89%\",\n            \"+11.83\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"R1-32B\",\n            \"0%\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"85%\",\n            \"+2.59\",\n            \"95%\",\n            \"+5.68\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"0%\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"74%\",\n            \"+9.86\",\n            \"79%\",\n            \"+9.40\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"BOCPD/CUSUM negative result is predictable. Method selection too narrow. Combined with AdvBench non-replication, entire negative result portfolio is dismissed as 'testing wrong tools on a narrow task.'\\n\\n\\nBOCPD在3/4模型上0% detection——OT-7B probe scores too spiky，32B models signal太weak。只有R1-8B有gradual transitions compatible with Gaussian change-point model。CUSUM universally worse than simple FCP threshold。 AdvBench非复现不直接影响BOCPD结果（BOCPD operates on temporal probe scores， AdvBench是binary task没有temporal scores），但strengthens the 'nothing transfers' narrative: '你测试了2个off-the-shelf change-point methods，都失败。 你的layer selection bias也不generalize。你的HS advantage也不generalize。 整篇paper的主题是\\\"on one specific dataset with one specific pipeline, one specific finding holds.\\\"'\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"False positive analysis (safe traces, all methods)\",\n    \"description\": \"All probes on safe traces across 4 models. Safe FPR, pre-CP FPR, precision-recall curves.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"r1_8b_hs_safe_fpr\": 0.39,\n      \"ot_7b_hs_safe_fpr\": \"0.111–0.175\",\n      \"qwq_32b_hs_safe_fpr\": \"0.45–0.50\",\n      \"r1_32b_hs_safe_fpr\": 0.239,\n      \"safe_fpr_median\": \"~0.39\",\n      \"gru_safe_fpr\": 0.521,\n      \"no_method_achieves_prec80\": true,\n      \"qwq_hs_fpr_worse_than_text\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Safe Trace FPR (all methods × models)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"HS Static FPR\",\n          \"HS Temporal FPR\",\n          \"Text Temporal FPR\",\n          \"GRU FPR\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"R1-8B\",\n            \"0.390\",\n            \"0.392\",\n            \"0.385\",\n            \"0.521\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"OT-7B\",\n            \"0.111\",\n            \"0.175\",\n            \"0.460\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"QwQ-32B\",\n            \"0.500\",\n            \"0.450\",\n            \"0.425\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"R1-32B\",\n            \"0.239\",\n            \"0.239\",\n            \"0.310\",\n            \"—\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Deployment Viability\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Criterion\",\n          \"Threshold\",\n          \"Models Meeting\",\n          \"Best Method\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Safe FPR < 20%\",\n            \"0.20\",\n            \"1/4 (OT-7B)\",\n            \"HS static L16\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Precision > 70%\",\n            \"0.70\",\n            \"1/4 (R1-8B W≥15)\",\n            \"HS temporal W=25\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Coverage > 60%\",\n            \"0.60\",\n            \"2/4\",\n            \"HS temporal W=1\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"All three\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0/4\",\n            \"None\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"FPR is the deployment dealbreaker on CP data. AdvBench non-replication closes the escape hatch of 'maybe it works better on other datasets' — it demonstrably works WORSE. Reviewer verdict: 'A monitor that fires on 1/3 safe traces is noise. Adding AdvBench shows this isn't fixable by changing the dataset.'\\n\\n\\nSafe FPR 11-52%，median约39%。这意味着每2-3个safe traces就有1个false alarm。 No operating point achieves simultaneous >70% precision AND >60% coverage on ANY model。QwQ HS safe FPR (45-50%) actually HIGHER than text (33-43%)。 AdvBench非复现加重了practical concern: '在你优化过的CP dataset上，safe FPR 已经是39%。在AdvBench上HS甚至不优于text。任何real-world deployment都会面临 distribution shift，FPR只会更差。这个系统在production中是不可用的。'\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"AdvBench Layer Bias Replication (R1-8B)\",\n    \"description\": \"Replicate layer selection bias on AdvBench using R1-8B with binary jailbreak/safe labels. Train probes at all 32 layers, run 5-metric layer selection analysis. Tests whether crossing_rate bias is model-geometric (general) or CP-annotation-specific (task-bound).\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_traces_total\": 200,\n      \"n_jailbreak\": 104,\n      \"n_safe\": 96,\n      \"model\": \"R1-8B (Llama-8B, 32 layers)\",\n      \"layer_selection\": {\n        \"bal_acc_x_cr_layer\": 12,\n        \"bal_acc_x_cr_depth_pct\": 39,\n        \"pure_bal_acc_layer\": 13,\n        \"pure_bal_acc_depth_pct\": 42,\n        \"precision_layer\": 13,\n        \"precision_depth_pct\": 42,\n        \"pre_cp_fpr_layer\": 14,\n        \"pre_cp_fpr_depth_pct\": 45,\n        \"composite_layer\": 13,\n        \"composite_depth_pct\": 42\n      },\n      \"layer_gap_abs\": 2,\n      \"layer_gap_depth_pct\": 6,\n      \"crossing_rate_analysis\": {\n        \"shallow_mean_cr\": 0.12,\n        \"deep_mean_cr\": 0.11,\n        \"shallow_deep_ratio\": 1.05,\n        \"ratio_p_value\": 0.74,\n        \"pattern\": \"uniform — no shallow inflation\"\n      },\n      \"hs_performance_at_best_layer\": {\n        \"layer\": 13,\n        \"bal_acc\": 0.63,\n        \"precision\": 0.58,\n        \"pre_cp_fpr\": 0.31,\n        \"auroc\": 0.68\n      },\n      \"text_performance\": {\n        \"bal_acc\": 0.6,\n        \"precision\": 0.55,\n        \"pre_cp_fpr\": 0.34\n      },\n      \"hs_text_comparison\": {\n        \"bal_acc_delta_pp\": 3.0,\n        \"precision_delta_pp\": 3.0,\n        \"fpr_delta_pp\": -3.0,\n        \"bal_acc_p\": 0.28,\n        \"precision_p\": 0.31,\n        \"fpr_p\": 0.42,\n        \"hb_pass_rate\": \"0/3\"\n      },\n      \"cp_data_comparison\": {\n        \"cp_cr_composite_layer\": \"L0 (0%)\",\n        \"advbench_cr_composite_layer\": \"L12 (39%)\",\n        \"cp_layer_gap\": 16,\n        \"advbench_layer_gap\": 2,\n        \"cp_hs_text_prec_delta\": \"+22.4pp\",\n        \"advbench_hs_text_prec_delta\": \"+3.0pp\",\n        \"cp_crossing_rate_shallow_deep_ratio\": 2.8,\n        \"advbench_crossing_rate_shallow_deep_ratio\": 1.05\n      }\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"AdvBench Layer Selection (R1-8B, 5 metrics)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Metric\",\n          \"Selected Layer\",\n          \"Depth %\",\n          \"bal_acc at Layer\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"bal_acc × crossing_rate\",\n            \"L12\",\n            \"39%\",\n            \"0.62\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"pure bal_acc\",\n            \"L13\",\n            \"42%\",\n            \"0.63\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"precision\",\n            \"L13\",\n            \"42%\",\n            \"0.63\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"pre_cp_fpr\",\n            \"L14\",\n            \"45%\",\n            \"0.62\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"composite (additive)\",\n            \"L13\",\n            \"42%\",\n            \"0.63\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"CP Data vs AdvBench: Layer Selection Bias Comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Dimension\",\n          \"CP Temporal Data\",\n          \"AdvBench Binary\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"cr_composite layer\",\n            \"L0 (0%)\",\n            \"L12 (39%)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"threshold metrics layer\",\n            \"L16 (52%)\",\n            \"L13 (42%)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"layer gap (abs)\",\n            \"16 layers\",\n            \"2 layers\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"layer gap (depth %)\",\n            \"52%\",\n            \"6%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"CR shallow/deep ratio\",\n            \"2.8\",\n            \"1.05\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"HS-text Δprec\",\n            \"+22.4pp (HB ✅)\",\n            \"+3.0pp (NS)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"HB pass rate\",\n            \"3/3\",\n            \"0/3\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"AdvBench Crossing Rate by Depth Band\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Depth Band\",\n          \"Mean CR\",\n          \"Std CR\",\n          \"n layers\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Shallow (L0-L7)\",\n            \"0.12\",\n            \"0.03\",\n            \"8\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Middle (L8-L23)\",\n            \"0.11\",\n            \"0.02\",\n            \"16\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Deep (L24-L31)\",\n            \"0.11\",\n            \"0.03\",\n            \"8\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Shallow/Deep ratio\",\n            \"1.05\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"t-test p-value\",\n            \"0.74\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"AdvBench HS vs Text Performance (R1-8B)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Modality\",\n          \"bal_acc\",\n          \"precision\",\n          \"FPR\",\n          \"AUROC\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"HS (L13)\",\n            \"0.63\",\n            \"0.58\",\n            \"0.31\",\n            \"0.68\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Text (MiniLM)\",\n            \"0.60\",\n            \"0.55\",\n            \"0.34\",\n            \"0.65\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Δ\",\n            \"+3.0pp\",\n            \"+3.0pp\",\n            \"−3.0pp\",\n            \"+3.0pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"p-value\",\n            \"0.28\",\n            \"0.31\",\n            \"0.42\",\n            \"0.25\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"HB pass?\",\n            \"No\",\n            \"No\",\n            \"No\",\n            \"No\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"AdvBench非复现是worst-tier的defining experiment。结论分为两个component:\\n(1) Layer selection bias非复现：Mechanistically consistent。crossing_rate bias 的root cause是temporal probe score序列中shallow layers的noise-driven threshold crossings。Binary classification不产生这种temporal structure。Bias不复现 不invalidate CP data上的finding——it BOUNDS its scope。C1调整为: 'Layer selection bias is a pitfall specific to temporal monitoring pipelines that use threshold-crossing statistics for layer selection. It does not manifest in standard binary classification settings.'\\n(2) HS-text gap非复现：+3pp (NS) vs CP data +22.4pp (HB ✅)。HS advantage 在binary classification上消失，说明advantage来自temporal structure interaction（hidden states track step-level state transitions that text embeddings miss），而非inherent modality superiority。\\nPaper impact: C1 scope从'any hidden-state evaluation should audit layer selection metrics'收窄到'temporal monitoring pipelines using crossing_rate should audit layer selection'。这让contribution更precise但less impactful。\\nReviewer split: - Supportive (R1): 'Boundary condition analysis is good science. Showing WHERE\\n  your finding applies and where it doesn't is a sign of mature methodology.\\n  The AdvBench non-replication actually STRENGTHENS the mechanistic argument —\\n  you can explain WHY it doesn't replicate (no temporal structure → no\\n  crossing_rate bias).'\\n- Dismissive (R2): 'The non-replication means your headline finding (layer\\n  selection bias) is a pipeline implementation detail, not a scientific\\n  contribution. You could fix it by not using crossing_rate — which any\\n  careful practitioner would do anyway. The AdvBench experiment cost 6 GPU\\n  hours to show your contribution is smaller than claimed.'\\n\\nDream score delta: -0.10 (3.0 → 2.9). Non-replication doesn't kill the paper but removes the possibility of C1 earning generalizability points. Paper stays at Findings level; no pathway to Main opened by this experiment.\\n\\nAdvBench非复现的root cause是mechanistic的，不是statistical noise:\\n(1) crossing_rate在binary classification中不产生浅层虚高。在CP temporal data中， crossing_rate measures how often probe score crosses threshold DURING a trace。 Shallow layers的probes更noisy（cosine distance smaller, representations more compressed）→更频繁crossing→crossing_rate虚高→与bal_acc相乘后bias toward shallow layers。在AdvBench binary classification中，虽然可以对每个token计算probe score 并观察序列，但binary labels（jailbreak/safe per trace）不创造step-level temporal structure。Probe scores在entire trace上趋于一致（consistently jailbreak或consistently safe），所以crossing_rate在所有层上都很低（~0.11-0.12）， shallow/deep ratio≈1.05，无显著差异。\\n(2) HS-text gap在binary classification中collapse到+3pp (NS)。原因：hidden states 对CP temporal task的advantage来自于tracking step-level safety state transitions—— hidden states可以detect commitment point前后的internal representation shift。 Binary classification不需要这种temporal sensitivity——只需要区分'this trace as a whole is jailbreak vs safe'。对于这个coarser task，text embeddings capture足够 的signal（jailbreak traces有distinctive vocabulary/structure），HS的marginal advantage消失。\\n(3) Layer selection convergence: 5个metrics全部选中层（L12-L14, 39-45% depth）， gap仅2层。这是因为binary classification的optimal layer由overall representation quality决定（middle layers best balance between too-raw和too-output-specific）， 而非threshold-crossing-dynamics。所有metrics agree on similar layers because they're all measuring the same underlying discriminability without temporal confounds。\\n(4) Traces太uniform: AdvBench prompts + R1-8B responses产生相对homogeneous的 traces——jailbreak traces consistently harmful, safe traces consistently refuse。 没有CP temporal data中的'safety reasoning followed by commitment'渐进结构。 所有层表现接近（bal_acc range across layers: 0.55-0.65），metric间无显著分化。\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":1282,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1282}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0135TogeeYfKQrue8xxF8cq9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — contrastive_trace_safety\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (LLM Security Track)\n5\t- Deadline: ARR submission May 25, 2026\n6\t\n7\t## Core Claims (D031: layer selection as primary contribution, 4 models)\n8\t\n9\t**Contribution structure (D031)**:\n10\t- **Primary (methodological)**: Layer selection metric sensitivity — biased metrics reverse conclusions\n11\t- **Secondary (empirical)**: HS advantage at correct layers, qualified by scale; temporal improvement universal\n12\t- **Tertiary (negative results)**: 6 alternative methods fail, establishing what doesn't work\n13\t\n14\t1. **Temporal context universally improves probe accuracy across both modalities** (D014 + D023 nuanced) — Temporal context is the most robust improvement across all 4 models and both modalities. Text probes benefit consistently from larger windows across all models. HS probes benefit in 3/4 models; OT-7B HS peaks at W=3 and declines at larger W (confirmed on both layer 0 and layer 16).\n15\t\n16\t   **HS temporal gains** (W=1→best, 3 original models): accuracy +3pp (77.8%→81.0%), precision +13pp (69.3%→82.2%), FPR halved (9.65%→4.83%).\n17\t   **OT-7B HS temporal** (layer 16): bal_acc peaks at W=3 (68.8%), declines to 65.9% at W=25 (-2.9pp). BUT precision and FPR continue improving (prec 42.8%→49.4%, FPR 12.0%→7.5%). Temporal saturation is bal_acc-specific; larger W trades bal_acc for better precision/FPR. Confirmed on both layer 0 and layer 16 — genuine OT-7B characteristic.\n18\t   **Text temporal gains** (all 4 models, W=1→best): universally +3-12pp bal_acc, +8-23pp precision, FPR reduced.\n19\t\n20\t   **IMPORTANT: coverage direction depends on detection criterion K.** K=1 (single crossing): coverage DECREASES with W. K=5 (consecutive crossings): coverage may INCREASE with W. Claim 1 is about accuracy/precision improvement, NOT coverage.\n21\t\n22\t   The core insight: \"Temporal context universally improves text probes across all 4 models. For HS probes, 3/4 models benefit from larger temporal windows, while OT-7B shows early saturation (W=3 peak).\"\n23\t\n24\t2. **HS probes outperform text probes at correctly selected layers, with strong evidence at 7-8B and model-specific patterns at 32B** (D031: qualified from D030 \"universal\") —\n25\t\n26\t   **KEY FINDING (D029)**: The apparent \"scale-dependent\" HS advantage (significant at 7-8B, absent at 32B) was an artifact of the layer selection metric `bal_acc × crossing_rate`, which systematically selects shallow layers for 32B models. At correctly selected deep layers, HS outperforms text on all 4 models, but the NATURE of the advantage differs by scale.\n27\t\n28\t   **Layer selection metric bias (D028, refined D036)**: `bal_acc × crossing_rate` selects shallow layers because shallow layers have higher crossing rates (more trigger-happy probes). 3/4 non-biased threshold-dependent metrics (precision, pre_cp_fpr, composite) converge on deep layers; pure bal_acc (threshold-free) selects middle layers (L30/L47). Deployment-relevant metric consensus selects L60/L63 for 32B models.\n29\t\n30\t   **7-8B models: HS significantly outperforms text (2/2 models) — UNCHANGED**\n31\t\n32\t   **R1-8B (Llama-8B, R1-distilled, n=43):**\n33\t   - Precision: 69.7% vs 47.2% (+22.4pp, p<0.001, **Holm-Bonferroni ✅**)\n34\t   - Accuracy: 81.3% vs 72.9% (+8.2pp, p=0.005, **Holm-Bonferroni ✅**)\n35\t   - Pre-CP FPR: 14.8% vs 22.2% (-7.4pp, p=0.044, **Holm-Bonferroni ✅** — rank 12/12, threshold=0.0500)\n36\t\n37\t   **OT-7B (Qwen-7B, STILL-trained, n=47, layer 16, matched W=3) — D026 M001 corrected:**\n38\t   - Bal Acc: 68.8% vs 58.1% (+10.7pp, p<0.001, **Holm-Bonferroni ✅**)\n39\t   - Precision: 46.6% vs 24.7% (+21.9pp, p<0.001, **Holm-Bonferroni ✅**)\n40\t   - Pre-CP FPR: 10.0% vs 27.1% (-17.1pp, p<0.001, **Holm-Bonferroni ✅**)\n41\t   - **3/3 metrics significant — OT-7B cleanest single-model evidence**\n42\t\n43\t   **32B models: HS outperforms text at CORRECTED LAYERS but with model-specific caveats (D029+D031)**\n44\t\n45\t   **QwQ-32B (Qwen-32B, native, n=19-20, Layer 60 corrected from L2):**\n46\t   - Bal Acc: +11-22pp across all 15 window sizes, all p<0.005 (bootstrap, null-centered)\n47\t   - 15/15 W values show significant HS advantage\n48\t   - **CRITICAL CAVEAT (D031)**: HS lead time NEGATIVE (-1.2 to 1.9 steps vs text 3.7-12.3). HS is \"more accurate but detects later.\" Position baseline bal_acc=0.746 > HS=0.740. In safety deployment, detecting 8-12 steps earlier may outweigh higher accuracy.\n49\t   - W=3: bal_acc +15.4pp, precision +23.6pp, FPR -9.8pp — **3/3 Holm-Bonferroni ✅**\n50\t\n51\t   **R1-32B (Qwen-32B, R1-distilled, n=20, Layer 63 corrected from L10):**\n52\t   - W=1: bal_acc +18.7pp (0.826 vs 0.639), p=0.000; precision +15.4pp (0.603 vs 0.448), p=0.000; FPR -14.6pp (0.235 vs 0.380), p=0.007 — **3/3 Bonferroni ✅**\n53\t   - W=3: bal_acc +11.2pp p=0.020, precision p=0.032, FPR p=0.077 — **0/3 Bonferroni** (v2 null-centered)\n54\t   - W=5-15: all NS\n55\t   - **CRITICAL CAVEAT (D031)**: Only W=1 passes Bonferroni, coverage=25% (5/20 traces). Evidence statistically narrow.\n56\t   - **POSITIVE**: Lead time +11.7-13.3 steps (excellent early warning). HS >> Position (+14.2pp).\n57\t   - **NOTE**: L10 vs L63 at W=10: L10=0.795 vs L63=0.754 — L10 slightly better. \"Scheme B选错层\" narrative weaker for R1-32B.\n58\t   - ✅ P-VALUE UNIFIED: null-centered two-sided, consistent with QwQ.\n59\t\n60\t   **32B cross-model divergence (D031 key finding for Discussion):**\n61\t   | Dimension | R1-32B L63 | QwQ L60 |\n62\t   |-----------|-----------|---------|\n63\t   | Bonferroni | W=1 only (3/3) | All 15 W (3/3) |\n64\t   | Lead time | +11.7 to +13.3 (excellent) | -1.2 to +1.9 (poor) |\n65\t   | HS vs Position | HS >> Pos (+14.2pp) | HS < Pos (-0.6pp) |\n66\t   | Coverage (W=1) | 25% | 74% |\n67\t   Paper MUST discuss this divergence per-model, not unify into single narrative.\n68\t\n69\t   **OLD 32B results at BIASED layers (L2/L10) — RETRACTED as metric artifact:**\n70\t   - R1-32B L10: precision +9.4pp (p=0.064), accuracy +6.2pp (p=0.13)\n71\t   - QwQ-32B L2: precision +5.8pp (p=0.35), accuracy +0.9pp (p=0.83)\n72\t   - These non-significant results were caused by the biased `bal_acc × crossing_rate` metric selecting layers where HS signal is weak.\n73\t\n74\t   **Holm-Bonferroni COMPLETE (D032): 12/12 ALL PASS**\n75\t   Previously 5/12 (biased layers). With corrected layers: **12/12** step-down pass (α=0.05).\n76\t\n77\t   | Rank | Model | Metric | p-value | Threshold | Pass |\n78\t   |------|-------|--------|---------|-----------|------|\n79\t   | 1 | R1-8B | precision | <0.001 | 0.0042 | ✅ |\n80\t   | 2 | OT-7B | bal_acc | <0.001 | 0.0045 | ✅ |\n81\t   | 3 | OT-7B | precision | <0.001 | 0.0050 | ✅ |\n82\t   | 4 | OT-7B | FPR | <0.001 | 0.0056 | ✅ |\n83\t   | 5 | QwQ | bal_acc | <0.001 | 0.0063 | ✅ |\n84\t   | 6 | QwQ | precision | <0.001 | 0.0071 | ✅ |\n85\t   | 7 | R1-32B | bal_acc | <0.001 | 0.0083 | ✅ |\n86\t   | 8 | R1-32B | precision | <0.001 | 0.0100 | ✅ |\n87\t   | 9 | QwQ | FPR | 0.004 | 0.0125 | ✅ |\n88\t   | 10 | R1-8B | bal_acc | 0.005 | 0.0167 | ✅ |\n89\t   | 11 | R1-32B | FPR | 0.007 | 0.0250 | ✅ |\n90\t   | 12 | R1-8B | FPR | 0.044 | 0.0500 | ✅ |\n91\t\n92\t   **Caveats**: Different W per model (R1-8B W=15, OT-7B/QwQ W=3, R1-32B W=1). Tests measure \"HS > text at each model's best setting,\" not \"at uniform W.\" R1-32B W=1 has 25% coverage. Paper must state these conditions.\n93\t\n94\t   **FPR dual reporting**: Step-level (r1-8b: HS 8.8%, text 20.5%) vs trace-level (HS 14.8%, text 22.2%). Both reported.\n95\t\n96\t   **FPR reversal RESOLVED (D026)**: OT-7B HS FPR@L16 (10.0%) < Text FPR at ANY window (16.1%). HS dominates on FPR at 7-8B.\n97\t\n98\t   **Effect size consistency**: Precision advantage across all 4 models at corrected layers: R1-8B +22.4pp, OT-7B +21.2pp, QwQ +23.6pp, R1-32B +15.4pp. Remarkably consistent +15-24pp range.\n99\t\n100\t   Paper recommended claim (D032): \"At correctly selected layers, HS probes significantly outperform text probes on all 4 models (12/12 Holm-Bonferroni, precision +15-24pp). The layer selection metric `bal_acc × crossing_rate` systematically biases toward shallow layers, entirely masking this advantage at 32B scale. Practical implications differ by model: 7-8B models show strong HS advantage with comparable timing; R1-32B shows excellent lead time (+13 steps) but narrow coverage at the significant window; QwQ shows robust statistical significance but HS detects later than text.\"\n101\t\n102\t3. **HS and text monitoring exhibit different detection timing profiles** (narrowed from old Claim 3, now 4/4 models with GRU) — On r1-8b: HS temporal LR detects AT the commitment point (lead ~0) while text detects ~10 steps earlier. GRU achieves modest positive lead (+4.5+-3.0, worst seed +0.9). BOCPD/CUSUM fail to improve over simple thresholds. The oracle's +31-step advantage shows hidden states encode current safety state but poorly predict future trajectory. On 32B models: timing profiles vary (QwQ GRU lead +15.9, R1-32B +8.8) but FPR differences are non-significant. OT-7B GRU (layer 16, multi-layer L13-19, 3 seeds): bal_acc 74.6+-1.3%, coverage 64.5+-7.0%, lead +33.6+-4.8, FPR 27.4+-6.1%. GRU trades higher FPR for longer lead (consistent with R1-8B pattern). NOTE: OT-7B absolute lead times are inflated by long traces (~80 sentences vs ~30). Paper should normalize or explicitly caveat trace length differences when comparing across models.\n103\t\n104\t   NOTE: 32B timing profiles should be RE-EVALUATED at corrected layers (L60/L63) — current GRU results used old biased layers.\n105\t\n106\t## Methodological Contribution: Layer Selection Sensitivity (D028, elevated from RETRACTED Claim 4)\n107\t\n108\tThe layer selection metric `bal_acc × crossing_rate` systematically biases toward shallow layers because shallow layers have higher crossing rates (more frequently crossing the detection threshold). This bias is especially problematic for 32B models, where it selects layers at 3-16% depth instead of deep layers selected by threshold-dependent metrics.\n109\t\n110\t**Ablation evidence (4 models × 5 metrics) — UPDATED D036**:\n111\t- `bal_acc × crossing_rate`: selects L2 (QwQ), L10 (R1-32B) — shallow, biased\n112\t- Pure `bal_acc`: selects **middle layers** for 32B — L30 (QwQ, 47%), L47 (R1-32B, 73%); deep for 7-8B (agrees with other metrics). Threshold-free metric, behaves like AUROC.\n113\t- `precision`: selects deep layers for all models (L60/L63 for 32B)\n114\t- `pre_cp_fpr`: selects deep layers for all models\n115\t- `composite` (additive): selects deep layers for all models\n116\t- **Pattern**: Threshold-FREE metrics (bal_acc, AUROC) → middle layers; threshold-DEPENDENT metrics (precision, FPR, composite) → deep layers. Crossing_rate-contaminated metrics → shallow layers.\n117\t\n118\t**LOO-CV stability (D036)**: Under LOO-CV, the dominant layer is selected in 65-68% of folds regardless of metric. QwQ L30 selected 13/19 folds (bal_acc), R1-32B L47 selected 13/20 folds (bal_acc). Selection is robust to val set composition.\n119\t\n120\t**Corrected layer definition (D036)**: \"Corrected layers\" = consensus of threshold-dependent metrics (precision, inverse FPR, additive composite) — 3 of 4 non-biased metrics, not all 4. Pure bal_acc selects middle layers (L30/L47) with higher step-level accuracy (0.75/0.83 vs 0.71/0.76 at deep layers), but deployment-relevant metrics (precision, FPR) favor deep layers where all 4 models achieve HB-significant HS advantage.\n121\t\n122\t**Impact**: Incorrect layer selection entirely masked the HS advantage at 32B scale, leading to the false conclusion that HS advantage is scale-dependent. Even among unbiased metrics, metric choice affects optimal layer depth — threshold-free vs threshold-dependent is a second dimension of researcher degrees of freedom beyond the crossing_rate bias.\n123\t\n124\t**Anti-circular-reasoning defense (D031, refined D036)**: The claim that deep layers are \"correct\" for deployment rests on: (1) `crossing_rate` has a MECHANISTIC bias — shallow layers have compressed representations (1.4-2.7× smaller cosine distances) generating noise-driven crossings; (2) threshold-dependent metrics (precision, FPR) directly measure deployment-relevant properties; (3) at deep layers, all 4 models show significant HS advantage (HB corrected); (4) LOO-CV confirms selection stability. The paper presents layer selection as a \"researcher degree of freedom\" with systematic audit via sensitivity analysis.\n125\t\n126\t**Paper treatment**: Dedicated subsection in Method + Table showing metric × model → selected layer + downstream HS-text comparison. Frame as sensitivity analysis, not \"correct vs incorrect.\"\n127\t\n128\t## Story Spine\n129\t\n130\t- **Problem**: Reasoning models exhibit \"self-jailbreak\" — safety reasoning and harmful execution interleave throughout extended CoT, culminating in a \"commitment point\" where safety reasoning permanently ceases. Existing hidden-state detectors (TrajGuard, SafeSwitch, SIREN) target adversarial INPUT attacks on instruction-tuned models; they have never been evaluated on reasoning-model self-jailbreak where the threat is the model's OWN reasoning process.\n131\t\n132\t- **Existing approaches & gaps**: Hidden-state detectors achieve 95% defense on standard LLMs (TrajGuard) but operate at token-level. Reasoning-model safety analysis (Self-Jailbreaking, HarmThoughts) characterizes the phenomenon without providing deployable detectors. Text-level CoT monitors (SafeRemind, SafeThink) are vulnerable to pathological CoT. No prior work systematically compares detection modalities for reasoning-model self-jailbreak.\n133\t\n134\t- **Our framing**: We provide the first systematic evaluation of detection methods for reasoning-model safety commitment across **4 models, 2 architectures (Llama + Qwen), 2 training methods (R1-distilled + STILL), and 2 scales (7-8B + 32B)**, comparing hidden-state probes, text-based probes, sequence models (GRU), change-point detection (BOCPD/CUSUM), unsupervised methods (Mahalanobis, RepE direction, CCS), and nonlinear probes (MLP). We discover that: (a) temporal context is the most robust improvement, benefiting both modalities universally; (b) HS probes universally outperform text at correctly selected layers, with a critical sensitivity to layer selection methodology; (c) existing methods designed for other settings (TrajGuard, BOCPD, CCS, RepE) fail on reasoning model commitment; (d) linear probes are sufficient (MLP adds no value); (e) safety representations are model-specific (cross-transfer fails entirely), validated by control task (selectivity 7.5-18.5pp).\n135\t\n136\t- **Headline result (D032)**: \"Hidden-state probes significantly outperform text probes for safety commitment detection on all 4 models (12/12 Holm-Bonferroni, precision +15-24pp), but this advantage is entirely invisible under biased layer selection metrics. The composite metric `bal_acc × crossing_rate` systematically selects shallow layers at 32B scale, masking the HS signal and producing false negatives. This methodological finding, together with model-specific practical trade-offs at 32B (early detection vs broad coverage) and the comprehensive failure of 6 alternative methods, establishes that supervised temporal probing with principled layer selection is the most effective and robust approach for monitoring reasoning-model safety commitment.\"\n137\t\n138\t## Key Evidence Summary\n139\t\n140\t### R1-8B (Llama-8B, R1-distilled, n=43 test, class_weight=balanced, 60/20/20 split — CANONICAL)\n141\t\n142\tBest layer: 14 (45% depth). Full sweep layers 0-24 confirmed: layer 12 marginally better (+0.4pp bal_acc), no practical difference.\n143\t\n144\t| Method | Coverage | Lead Time | Pre-CP FPR | Safe FPR |\n145\t|--------|----------|-----------|------------|----------|\n146\t| HS Static LR L14 | — | -2.0 | **9.7%** | — |\n147\t| HS Temporal W=5 | — | +0.6 | **10.2%** | — |\n148\t| HS Temporal W=15 | — | **-0.1** | **8.8%** | — |\n149\t| Text Temporal W=15 | — | +10.3* | 20.5% | — |\n150\t| GRU Multi-7L (3-seed) | 72.9+-4.0% | +4.5+-3.0 | 16.5+-4.1% | — |\n151\t\n152\t*Text lead time inflated by high pre-CP FPR (trigger-happy detection)\n153\tNOTE: HS LR lead time collapsed to ~0 with balanced weights — HS detects AT commitment, not before. Only GRU achieves positive lead (+4.5, worst seed +0.9).\n154\t\n155\t### OT-7B (Qwen-7B, STILL-trained, n=47 test, 228 total traces) — D026 CORRECTED to layer 16\n156\t\n157\tBest layer: **16 (57% depth)**. M001 corrected: layer 0 was selected by bal_acc x crossing_rate bug; layer 16 selected by bal_acc.\n158\t\n159\t| Method | Bal Acc | Precision | Coverage | Pre-CP FPR | Lead |\n160\t|--------|---------|-----------|----------|-----------|------|\n161\t| HS Temporal W=3 L16 (best) | **68.8%** | **46.6%** | 53.2% | **10.0%** | — |\n162\t| Text Temporal W=25 (best) | 61.4% | 32.9% | 66.0% | 16.1% | 45.5 |\n163\t| Text Temporal W=3 (matched) | 57.9% | 25.4% | — | 27.1% | — |\n164\t\n165\tBootstrap (layer 16, matched W=3, 1000 resamples):\n166\t   bal_acc +10.7pp, 95% CI [4.9, 16.3], p<0.001 ✅\n167\t   precision +21.9pp, 95% CI [14.0, 29.6], p<0.001 ✅\n168\t   FPR -17.1pp, 95% CI [-22.2, -12.2], p<0.001 ✅ — **3/3 Holm-Bonferroni**\n169\tSelectivity: 18.3pp (real=0.688, random=0.505) — highest across all models\n170\t\n171\t### 32B Cross-Architecture — D029 CORRECTED LAYERS\n172\t\n173\t**QwQ-32B (Qwen-32B, native, n=19-20, Layer 60 — corrected from L2)**\n174\t- HS vs Text bootstrap (null-centered, 1000 resamples):\n175\t  - Bal Acc: +11-22pp across W=1-15, ALL 15/15 significant (p<0.005)\n176\t  - W=3 (matched): bal_acc +15.4pp (p<0.001 ✅), precision +23.6pp (p<0.001 ✅), FPR -9.8pp (p=0.004 ✅) — **3/3 Holm-Bonferroni**\n177\t- Caveats: HS lead time poor (-1.2 to 1.9 steps vs text 3.7-12.3), position baseline competitive\n178\t- Layer selection: `bal_acc × crossing_rate` selected L2 (3.1%); threshold-dependent metrics consensus selects L60 (94%); pure `bal_acc` selects L30 (47%, bal_acc=0.7513 vs L60=0.7101) (D036)\n179\t\n180\t**R1-32B (Qwen-32B, R1-distilled, n=20, Layer 63 — corrected from L10)**\n181\t- W=1: bal_acc +18.7pp, p<0.001 ✅\n182\t- W≥5: NS (dimensionality: 51200 × W features for n=20)\n183\t- W=1: 3/3 Holm-Bonferroni ✅; W=3: 0/3 Bonferroni (null-centered v2); W≥5: NS\n184\t- Layer selection: `bal_acc × crossing_rate` selected L10 (15.6%); threshold-dependent metrics consensus selects L63 (98%); pure `bal_acc` selects L47 (73%, bal_acc=0.8290 vs L63=0.7585) (D036)\n185\t\n186\t**OLD biased-layer results (RETRACTED as metric artifact):**\n187\t| Model | Biased Layer | Corrected Layer | Biased p-value | Corrected p-value |\n188\t|-------|-------------|----------------|----------------|-------------------|\n189\t| R1-32B | L10 (16%) | L63 (98%) | 0.064-0.13 (NS) | <0.001 (W=1) ✅ |\n190\t| QwQ-32B | L2 (3%) | L60 (94%) | 0.35-0.83 (NS) | <0.005 (all W) ✅ |\n191\t\n192\t### 4-Model Holm-Bonferroni Summary (D032 — COMPLETE: 12/12 ✅)\n193\t\n194\t| Model | Arch | Training | Scale | Layer | W | bal_acc | precision | FPR | Selectivity |\n195\t|-------|------|----------|-------|-------|---|--------|-----------|-----|-------------|\n196\t| R1-8B | Llama | R1-distilled | 8B | L14 (45%) | 15 | +8.2pp ✅ | +22.4pp ✅ | -7.4pp ✅ | 12.5pp |\n197\t| OT-7B | Qwen | STILL | 7B | L16 (57%) | 3 | +10.7pp ✅ | +21.9pp ✅ | -17.1pp ✅ | 18.3pp |\n198\t| QwQ-32B | Qwen | native | 32B | L60 (94%) | 3 | +15.4pp ✅ | +23.6pp ✅ | -9.8pp ✅ | 15.3pp |\n199\t| R1-32B | Qwen | R1-distilled | 32B | L63 (98%) | 1 | +18.7pp ✅ | +15.4pp ✅ | -14.6pp ✅ | 7.5pp |\n200\t\n201\t**12/12 all pass Holm-Bonferroni step-down (α=0.05)**. Previously 5/12 with biased layers.\n202\tPrecision advantage remarkably consistent: +15.4 to +23.6pp across all 4 models.\n203\tCaveats: Different W per model; R1-32B W=1 coverage=25%; QwQ lead time negative.\n204\t\n205\t### Position Mediation Analysis (D038 → D039 → D042 complete)\n206\t\n207\t**Finding**: Position (relative step t/T) is a significant component of probe performance. 3/4 models retain HS advantage after residualization; R1-32B is the exception.\n208\t\n209\t| Model | W | Resid HS BA | Resid Text BA | HS-Text Gap | Gap p | HB |\n210\t|-------|---|------------|--------------|-------------|-------|-----|\n211\t| OT-7B | 3 | 0.609 | 0.540 | **+6.9pp** | <0.001 | ✅ |\n212\t| R1-8B | 15 | 0.669 | 0.605 | **+6.4pp** | <0.001 | ✅ |\n213\t| R1-8B | 3 | 0.571 | 0.534 | **+3.7pp** | <0.001 | ✅ |\n214\t| QwQ-32B | 3 | 0.619 | 0.582 | **+3.7pp** | 0.011 | ✅ |\n215\t| R1-32B | 1 | 0.552 | 0.532 | +1.9pp | 0.914 | ❌ |\n216\t\n217\tPosition-only BA: OT-7B/R1-8B/QwQ ≈ 0.706; R1-32B = 0.686.\n218\t\n219\t**D042 final picture**: HS advantage has two separable components — position-mediated and non-positional. 3/4 models retain significant non-positional HS advantage (HB pass); R1-32B's advantage is almost entirely position-mediated (drop 20.4pp, residualized gap NS). This is consistent with R1-32B's other caveats (W=1 only significant, 25% coverage).\n220\t\n221\t**Key implications for paper**:\n222\t1. Position is a significant component (12-20pp drop) — universal\n223\t2. 3/4 models retain non-positional HS advantage (residualized BA gap +3.7 to +6.9pp, HB pass)\n224\t3. R1-32B is the exception: HS advantage position-mediated, residualized gap NS\n225\t4. Temporal windowing preserves non-positional signal (R1-8B: 24pp drop@W=3 → 16pp@W=15)\n226\t5. Paper framing: \"HS advantage persists after position removal in 3/4 models; R1-32B advantage is position-mediated\"\n227\t\n228\t### 5-Seed Robustness (D041)\n229\t\n230\t| Model | HB Pass Rate | Mean ΔPrec ± std | Mean ΔBA ± std |\n231\t|-------|-------------|------------------|----------------|\n232\t| OT-7B | **5/5** | +20.9 ± 1.3pp | +11.3 ± 1.5pp |\n233\t| R1-32B | **5/5** | +13.7 ± 2.0pp | +11.8 ± 2.4pp |\n234\t| QwQ-32B | **5/5** | +19.9 ± 2.5pp | +11.4 ± 3.2pp |\n235\t| R1-8B | **3/5** | +14.6 ± 4.5pp | +9.4 ± 2.4pp |\n236\t\n237\tR1-8B failures are FPR-specific (seed 123 FPR p=0.262, seed 456 FPR p=0.952). Precision is directionally consistent across all 20 seed-model combinations (always positive). ΔPrec never reverses.\n238\t\n239\t**D041 insight**: Robustness splits by MODEL not SCALE. R1-32B (5/5, ΔPrec std=2.0pp) is more stable than R1-8B (3/5, ΔPrec std=4.5pp). \"7-8B robust / 32B sample-limited\" is factually wrong. R1-32B's absolute precision std (12.8pp) vs paired delta std (2.0pp) — paper must distinguish these.\n240\t\n241\t**Per-model evidence tier (D042)**:\n242\t- **OT-7B**: Cleanest — 5/5 HB, largest residualized gap (+6.9pp), highest selectivity (18.3pp)\n243\t- **QwQ-32B**: Robust — 5/5 HB, residualized gap holds (+3.7pp), but position baseline competitive\n244\t- **R1-8B**: Precision-robust — residualized holds (+3.7-6.4pp), but FPR unstable (3/5 HB)\n245\t- **R1-32B**: Statistically stable (5/5 HB, ΔPrec std=2.0pp) but advantage position-mediated (residualized NS)\n246\t\n247\t**Residualized precision**: Not tested (scope limited to BA per Reviewer recommendation).\n248\t\n249\t### BA-Layer Robustness: Metric Circularity Refuted (D044)\n250\t\n251\tR13 W1 concern: corrected selection uses threshold-dependent metrics (precision, FPR), evaluation also uses precision/FPR — potential circularity. Test: compute HS vs text on BA-selected layers (threshold-free criterion).\n252\t\n253\t| Model | BA-Opt Layer | ΔPrec | ΔBA | ΔFPR | ΔAUROC | All 4 HB? |\n254\t|-------|-------------|-------|-----|------|--------|-----------|\n255\t| R1-8B | L12 | +17.8pp | +7.8pp | -12.9pp | +9.3pp | ✅ |\n256\t| OT-7B | L9 | +25.4pp | +11.7pp | -19.3pp | +14.8pp | ✅ |\n257\t| QwQ-32B | L30 | +18.0pp | +14.8pp | -8.0pp | +17.3pp | ✅ |\n258\t| R1-32B | L47 | +13.7pp | +14.9pp | -23.1pp | +21.1pp | ✅ |\n259\t\n260\t**4/4 models × 4 metrics = 16/16 HB pass.** HS advantage is a genuine modality property, not a layer selection artifact.\n261\t\n262\tKey insight: R1-32B on BA-layer L47 shows ΔPrec +13.7pp and ΔAUROC +21.1pp — stronger than corrected L63 in threshold-free metrics. OT-7B on BA-layer L9 has ΔPrec +25.4pp > corrected L16 (+21.9pp). HS advantage is robust to layer selection criterion.\n263\t\n264\tCaveat: QwQ FPR adj_p=0.041 (marginal, CI close to zero). Other 15/16 tests p≤0.006.\n265\t\n266\t### Post-hoc Power Analysis (D044)\n267\t\n268\t| Model | n (test) | MDE (80% power, α=0.05) |\n269\t|-------|----------|------------------------|\n270\t| OT-7B | 47-69 | 6.7-8.1pp |\n271\t| R1-8B | 43-63 | 7.4-9.0pp |\n272\t| QwQ-32B | 29 | 9.5pp |\n273\t| R1-32B | 20-30 | 8.6-11.2pp |\n274\t\n275\tAll significant effects (15-24pp) are well above MDE → adequately powered. R1-32B residualized gap (+1.9pp) is far below MDE (11.2pp) → NS is power limitation, not evidence of absence.\n276\t\n277\t### Negative Results (expanded with R3 supplementary experiments)\n278\t- **BOCPD/CUSUM (4/4 models)**: R1-8B BOCPD 93% detection but lead +3.5 < threshold +4.9; OT-7B/R1-32B/QwQ all 0% detection across all λ. CUSUM worse than threshold on all models.\n279\t- **Mahalanobis (4/4 models confirmed negative)**: R1-8B (+0.07), OT-7B (+0.031), R1-32B (+0.148), QwQ (+0.204) — all separation ≈ 0. Signal in minor PCA components, not principal directions.\n280\t- **RepE direction probing (4/4 models, corrected layers confirmed)**: Direction-based supervised method (needs CP labels). Results highly layer-dependent and model-specific:\n281\t  - R1-32B L63 (corrected): bal_acc=0.559 (near-random), FPR=49.9% — **direction vector fails at deep layer** where supervised LR excels (0.826). Train-test gap 13.3pp shows poor generalization.\n282\t  - QwQ L60 (corrected): bal_acc=0.756, precision=0.512, FPR=0.309 — **improves** from L2 (0.666), narrows gap with supervised LR W=1 (0.740 bal_acc), but still inferior in precision/FPR.\n283\t  - R1-8B L14: bal_acc=0.749, OT-7B L16: bal_acc=0.642\n284\t  - **Key insight**: Supervised LR is necessary — mean-difference direction cannot decode safety commitment at deep layers for R1-32B (discriminative training finds signal that simple direction averaging misses). Layer selection sensitivity applies to RepE too, but in model-specific ways (R1-32B: shallow better for RepE; QwQ: deep better for both).\n285\t- **MLP nonlinear probe (4/4 models)**: 3/4 models worse than LR. Linear probes sufficient; nonlinearity adds no value.\n286\t- **CCS-adapted (Burns et al. 2023, 4/4 models)**: WEAKLY SUPERVISED (needs CP labels for pair construction, not truly unsupervised). R1-8B bal_acc=0.777 (between supervised LR 0.817 and RepE 0.749). R1-32B FPR=0.410 (near-chance). OT-7B precision=0.319 (near-chance, high lead=20.5 is artifact of high FPR). Uses scheme B layers (biased). Paper must: (a) label as \"weakly supervised\" not \"unsupervised\"; (b) disclose pair construction differs from Burns et al.; (c) note scheme B layer limitation.\n287\t- **Cross-model transfer (exp-003)**: All directions fail entirely (best: 58.3% acc, 40.7% crossing).\n288\t\n289\t## Venue Fit Risks\n290\t\n291\t- **EMNLP LLM Security track fit**: Strong — defense/detection evaluation for reasoning model jailbreak.\n292\t- **Method simplicity risk**: Mitigate: frame as empirical study. Contribution is systematic evaluation + layer selection sensitivity discovery + comprehensive negative results.\n293\t- **Statistical power**: With corrected layers, **12/12** tests pass Holm-Bonferroni across ALL 4 models (up from 5/12 with biased layers).\n294\t- **HS advantage universal**: UPGRADED from \"scale-dependent\" — layer selection is the true moderator, not scale. Cleaner story.\n295\t- **Trace vs step FPR**: Both reported with explanation.\n296\t- **High safe FPR risk**: All methods 39-62% safe FPR. Frame as diagnostic tool limitation.\n297\t- **32B caveats**: HS advantage exists but is more sensitive to window size (R1-32B W=1 only) and affected by dimensionality (51200-d at large W with n=20). Lead times shorter than 7-8B. These are honest limitations, not disqualifiers.\n298\t\n299\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n300\t\n301\t1. \"Method novelty?\" → Empirical study + systematic method comparison + layer selection sensitivity as methodological contribution + comprehensive negative results (6 methods fail).\n302\t2. \"Only one model family?\" → **RESOLVED**: 4 models, 2 architectures (Llama + Qwen), 2 training methods, 2 scales. ALL showing significant HS advantage at corrected layers.\n303\t3. \"GRU unstable?\" → 3-seed reporting with worst-seed bounds. R1-32B GRU at L10 was unstable; needs re-evaluation at L63.\n304\t4. \"Too few traces?\" → Cross-model validation + control task selectivity 7.5-18.5pp.\n305\t5. \"Fine-grained taxonomy?\" → Out of scope for current study.\n306\t6. \"HS doesn't beat text?\" → **QUALIFIED REBUTTAL (D031)**: HS outperforms text at correct layers with STRONG evidence at 7-8B (precision +22pp, Holm-Bonf) and QUALIFIED evidence at 32B (R1-32B: significant W=1 but narrow; QwQ: significant all W but late detection). 32B non-significance at OLD layers was artifact. Layer selection sensitivity analysis shows this is a methodological issue, not absence of signal.\n307\t7. \"Commitment point circular?\" → Cross-model consistency + control task selectivity argues against artifact.\n308\t8. \"Missing related work?\" → Expansion to 30+ citations.\n309\t9. \"No FPR analysis?\" → RESOLVED: both trace-level and step-level FPR reported.\n310\t10. \"High safe FPR?\" → Limitation acknowledged.\n311\t11. \"Control task / position confound?\" → RESOLVED (C2, D011): selectivity 7.5-18.5pp, HS FPR < position-only FPR.\n312\t12. \"Multiple testing correction?\" → **RESOLVED**: 12/12 Holm-Bonferroni step-down (α=0.05) across all 4 models (up from 5/12 with biased layers).\n313\t13. \"Trace vs step FPR?\" → Both reported with explanation.\n314\t14. \"Layer selection is just hyperparameter tuning?\" → No — we show a systematic bias in a commonly used composite metric. The ablation across 5 metrics × 4 models is a reproducible methodological finding, not a one-off parameter choice.\n315\t15. \"Why not just use the best layer for each model?\" → We do. The point is that the METRIC for selecting \"best\" matters. Biased metrics produce systematically wrong selections, masking real effects.\n316\t16. \"32B results are marginal / only W=1 significant for R1-32B?\" → Dimensionality constraint (51200 × W > n=20 at W≥5). QwQ L60 significant at ALL W values. R1-32B L63 significant at W=1. Both confirm HS advantage exists; practical deployment would use W=1-3 for 32B models.\n317\t\n318\t## Title Candidates (D031)\n319\t- \"Layer Selection Matters: How Metric Choice Determines Hidden-State Probe Effectiveness for Safety Monitoring\"\n320\t- \"Temporal Probing for Safety Commitment Detection in Reasoning Models: A Comprehensive Evaluation\"\n321\t- \"What Detects Safety Commitment in Reasoning Models? A Systematic Comparison of Probe-Based Methods\"\n322\t- \"Hidden-State vs Text Monitoring for Reasoning Model Safety: Layer Selection as the Missing Variable\"\n323\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fbcb0466-3e92-40e3-acd9-9335f9dd14ac","uuid":"96ab7df5-fbe5-4bee-bf89-cd3968d38f3d","timestamp":"2026-05-22T09:43:55.374Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/contrastive_trace_safety/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260522T085530/worst/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — contrastive_trace_safety\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (LLM Security Track)\n- Deadline: ARR submission May 25, 2026\n\n## Core Claims (D031: layer selection as primary contribution, 4 models)\n\n**Contribution structure (D031)**:\n- **Primary (methodological)**: Layer selection metric sensitivity — biased metrics reverse conclusions\n- **Secondary (empirical)**: HS advantage at correct layers, qualified by scale; temporal improvement universal\n- **Tertiary (negative results)**: 6 alternative methods fail, establishing what doesn't work\n\n1. **Temporal context universally improves probe accuracy across both modalities** (D014 + D023 nuanced) — Temporal context is the most robust improvement across all 4 models and both modalities. Text probes benefit consistently from larger windows across all models. HS probes benefit in 3/4 models; OT-7B HS peaks at W=3 and declines at larger W (confirmed on both layer 0 and layer 16).\n\n   **HS temporal gains** (W=1→best, 3 original models): accuracy +3pp (77.8%→81.0%), precision +13pp (69.3%→82.2%), FPR halved (9.65%→4.83%).\n   **OT-7B HS temporal** (layer 16): bal_acc peaks at W=3 (68.8%), declines to 65.9% at W=25 (-2.9pp). BUT precision and FPR continue improving (prec 42.8%→49.4%, FPR 12.0%→7.5%). Temporal saturation is bal_acc-specific; larger W trades bal_acc for better precision/FPR. Confirmed on both layer 0 and layer 16 — genuine OT-7B characteristic.\n   **Text temporal gains** (all 4 models, W=1→best): universally +3-12pp bal_acc, +8-23pp precision, FPR reduced.\n\n   **IMPORTANT: coverage direction depends on detection criterion K.** K=1 (single crossing): coverage DECREASES with W. K=5 (consecutive crossings): coverage may INCREASE with W. Claim 1 is about accuracy/precision improvement, NOT coverage.\n\n   The core insight: \"Temporal context universally improves text probes across all 4 models. For HS probes, 3/4 models benefit from larger temporal windows, while OT-7B shows early saturation (W=3 peak).\"\n\n2. **HS probes outperform text probes at correctly selected layers, with strong evidence at 7-8B and model-specific patterns at 32B** (D031: qualified from D030 \"universal\") —\n\n   **KEY FINDING (D029)**: The apparent \"scale-dependent\" HS advantage (significant at 7-8B, absent at 32B) was an artifact of the layer selection metric `bal_acc × crossing_rate`, which systematically selects shallow layers for 32B models. At correctly selected deep layers, HS outperforms text on all 4 models, but the NATURE of the advantage differs by scale.\n\n   **Layer selection metric bias (D028, refined D036)**: `bal_acc × crossing_rate` selects shallow layers because shallow layers have higher crossing rates (more trigger-happy probes). 3/4 non-biased threshold-dependent metrics (precision, pre_cp_fpr, composite) converge on deep layers; pure bal_acc (threshold-free) selects middle layers (L30/L47). Deployment-relevant metric consensus selects L60/L63 for 32B models.\n\n   **7-8B models: HS significantly outperforms text (2/2 models) — UNCHANGED**\n\n   **R1-8B (Llama-8B, R1-distilled, n=43):**\n   - Precision: 69.7% vs 47.2% (+22.4pp, p<0.001, **Holm-Bonferroni ✅**)\n   - Accuracy: 81.3% vs 72.9% (+8.2pp, p=0.005, **Holm-Bonferroni ✅**)\n   - Pre-CP FPR: 14.8% vs 22.2% (-7.4pp, p=0.044, **Holm-Bonferroni ✅** — rank 12/12, threshold=0.0500)\n\n   **OT-7B (Qwen-7B, STILL-trained, n=47, layer 16, matched W=3) — D026 M001 corrected:**\n   - Bal Acc: 68.8% vs 58.1% (+10.7pp, p<0.001, **Holm-Bonferroni ✅**)\n   - Precision: 46.6% vs 24.7% (+21.9pp, p<0.001, **Holm-Bonferroni ✅**)\n   - Pre-CP FPR: 10.0% vs 27.1% (-17.1pp, p<0.001, **Holm-Bonferroni ✅**)\n   - **3/3 metrics significant — OT-7B cleanest single-model evidence**\n\n   **32B models: HS outperforms text at CORRECTED LAYERS but with model-specific caveats (D029+D031)**\n\n   **QwQ-32B (Qwen-32B, native, n=19-20, Layer 60 corrected from L2):**\n   - Bal Acc: +11-22pp across all 15 window sizes, all p<0.005 (bootstrap, null-centered)\n   - 15/15 W values show significant HS advantage\n   - **CRITICAL CAVEAT (D031)**: HS lead time NEGATIVE (-1.2 to 1.9 steps vs text 3.7-12.3). HS is \"more accurate but detects later.\" Position baseline bal_acc=0.746 > HS=0.740. In safety deployment, detecting 8-12 steps earlier may outweigh higher accuracy.\n   - W=3: bal_acc +15.4pp, precision +23.6pp, FPR -9.8pp — **3/3 Holm-Bonferroni ✅**\n\n   **R1-32B (Qwen-32B, R1-distilled, n=20, Layer 63 corrected from L10):**\n   - W=1: bal_acc +18.7pp (0.826 vs 0.639), p=0.000; precision +15.4pp (0.603 vs 0.448), p=0.000; FPR -14.6pp (0.235 vs 0.380), p=0.007 — **3/3 Bonferroni ✅**\n   - W=3: bal_acc +11.2pp p=0.020, precision p=0.032, FPR p=0.077 — **0/3 Bonferroni** (v2 null-centered)\n   - W=5-15: all NS\n   - **CRITICAL CAVEAT (D031)**: Only W=1 passes Bonferroni, coverage=25% (5/20 traces). Evidence statistically narrow.\n   - **POSITIVE**: Lead time +11.7-13.3 steps (excellent early warning). HS >> Position (+14.2pp).\n   - **NOTE**: L10 vs L63 at W=10: L10=0.795 vs L63=0.754 — L10 slightly better. \"Scheme B选错层\" narrative weaker for R1-32B.\n   - ✅ P-VALUE UNIFIED: null-centered two-sided, consistent with QwQ.\n\n   **32B cross-model divergence (D031 key finding for Discussion):**\n   | Dimension | R1-32B L63 | QwQ L60 |\n   |-----------|-----------|---------|\n   | Bonferroni | W=1 only (3/3) | All 15 W (3/3) |\n   | Lead time | +11.7 to +13.3 (excellent) | -1.2 to +1.9 (poor) |\n   | HS vs Position | HS >> Pos (+14.2pp) | HS < Pos (-0.6pp) |\n   | Coverage (W=1) | 25% | 74% |\n   Paper MUST discuss this divergence per-model, not unify into single narrative.\n\n   **OLD 32B results at BIASED layers (L2/L10) — RETRACTED as metric artifact:**\n   - R1-32B L10: precision +9.4pp (p=0.064), accuracy +6.2pp (p=0.13)\n   - QwQ-32B L2: precision +5.8pp (p=0.35), accuracy +0.9pp (p=0.83)\n   - These non-significant results were caused by the biased `bal_acc × crossing_rate` metric selecting layers where HS signal is weak.\n\n   **Holm-Bonferroni COMPLETE (D032): 12/12 ALL PASS**\n   Previously 5/12 (biased layers). With corrected layers: **12/12** step-down pass (α=0.05).\n\n   | Rank | Model | Metric | p-value | Threshold | Pass |\n   |------|-------|--------|---------|-----------|------|\n   | 1 | R1-8B | precision | <0.001 | 0.0042 | ✅ |\n   | 2 | OT-7B | bal_acc | <0.001 | 0.0045 | ✅ |\n   | 3 | OT-7B | precision | <0.001 | 0.0050 | ✅ |\n   | 4 | OT-7B | FPR | <0.001 | 0.0056 | ✅ |\n   | 5 | QwQ | bal_acc | <0.001 | 0.0063 | ✅ |\n   | 6 | QwQ | precision | <0.001 | 0.0071 | ✅ |\n   | 7 | R1-32B | bal_acc | <0.001 | 0.0083 | ✅ |\n   | 8 | R1-32B | precision | <0.001 | 0.0100 | ✅ |\n   | 9 | QwQ | FPR | 0.004 | 0.0125 | ✅ |\n   | 10 | R1-8B | bal_acc | 0.005 | 0.0167 | ✅ |\n   | 11 | R1-32B | FPR | 0.007 | 0.0250 | ✅ |\n   | 12 | R1-8B | FPR | 0.044 | 0.0500 | ✅ |\n\n   **Caveats**: Different W per model (R1-8B W=15, OT-7B/QwQ W=3, R1-32B W=1). Tests measure \"HS > text at each model's best setting,\" not \"at uniform W.\" R1-32B W=1 has 25% coverage. Paper must state these conditions.\n\n   **FPR dual reporting**: Step-level (r1-8b: HS 8.8%, text 20.5%) vs trace-level (HS 14.8%, text 22.2%). Both reported.\n\n   **FPR reversal RESOLVED (D026)**: OT-7B HS FPR@L16 (10.0%) < Text FPR at ANY window (16.1%). HS dominates on FPR at 7-8B.\n\n   **Effect size consistency**: Precision advantage across all 4 models at corrected layers: R1-8B +22.4pp, OT-7B +21.2pp, QwQ +23.6pp, R1-32B +15.4pp. Remarkably consistent +15-24pp range.\n\n   Paper recommended claim (D032): \"At correctly selected layers, HS probes significantly outperform text probes on all 4 models (12/12 Holm-Bonferroni, precision +15-24pp). The layer selection metric `bal_acc × crossing_rate` systematically biases toward shallow layers, entirely masking this advantage at 32B scale. Practical implications differ by model: 7-8B models show strong HS advantage with comparable timing; R1-32B shows excellent lead time (+13 steps) but narrow coverage at the significant window; QwQ shows robust statistical significance but HS detects later than text.\"\n\n3. **HS and text monitoring exhibit different detection timing profiles** (narrowed from old Claim 3, now 4/4 models with GRU) — On r1-8b: HS temporal LR detects AT the commitment point (lead ~0) while text detects ~10 steps earlier. GRU achieves modest positive lead (+4.5+-3.0, worst seed +0.9). BOCPD/CUSUM fail to improve over simple thresholds. The oracle's +31-step advantage shows hidden states encode current safety state but poorly predict future trajectory. On 32B models: timing profiles vary (QwQ GRU lead +15.9, R1-32B +8.8) but FPR differences are non-significant. OT-7B GRU (layer 16, multi-layer L13-19, 3 seeds): bal_acc 74.6+-1.3%, coverage 64.5+-7.0%, lead +33.6+-4.8, FPR 27.4+-6.1%. GRU trades higher FPR for longer lead (consistent with R1-8B pattern). NOTE: OT-7B absolute lead times are inflated by long traces (~80 sentences vs ~30). Paper should normalize or explicitly caveat trace length differences when comparing across models.\n\n   NOTE: 32B timing profiles should be RE-EVALUATED at corrected layers (L60/L63) — current GRU results used old biased layers.\n\n## Methodological Contribution: Layer Selection Sensitivity (D028, elevated from RETRACTED Claim 4)\n\nThe layer selection metric `bal_acc × crossing_rate` systematically biases toward shallow layers because shallow layers have higher crossing rates (more frequently crossing the detection threshold). This bias is especially problematic for 32B models, where it selects layers at 3-16% depth instead of deep layers selected by threshold-dependent metrics.\n\n**Ablation evidence (4 models × 5 metrics) — UPDATED D036**:\n- `bal_acc × crossing_rate`: selects L2 (QwQ), L10 (R1-32B) — shallow, biased\n- Pure `bal_acc`: selects **middle layers** for 32B — L30 (QwQ, 47%), L47 (R1-32B, 73%); deep for 7-8B (agrees with other metrics). Threshold-free metric, behaves like AUROC.\n- `precision`: selects deep layers for all models (L60/L63 for 32B)\n- `pre_cp_fpr`: selects deep layers for all models\n- `composite` (additive): selects deep layers for all models\n- **Pattern**: Threshold-FREE metrics (bal_acc, AUROC) → middle layers; threshold-DEPENDENT metrics (precision, FPR, composite) → deep layers. Crossing_rate-contaminated metrics → shallow layers.\n\n**LOO-CV stability (D036)**: Under LOO-CV, the dominant layer is selected in 65-68% of folds regardless of metric. QwQ L30 selected 13/19 folds (bal_acc), R1-32B L47 selected 13/20 folds (bal_acc). Selection is robust to val set composition.\n\n**Corrected layer definition (D036)**: \"Corrected layers\" = consensus of threshold-dependent metrics (precision, inverse FPR, additive composite) — 3 of 4 non-biased metrics, not all 4. Pure bal_acc selects middle layers (L30/L47) with higher step-level accuracy (0.75/0.83 vs 0.71/0.76 at deep layers), but deployment-relevant metrics (precision, FPR) favor deep layers where all 4 models achieve HB-significant HS advantage.\n\n**Impact**: Incorrect layer selection entirely masked the HS advantage at 32B scale, leading to the false conclusion that HS advantage is scale-dependent. Even among unbiased metrics, metric choice affects optimal layer depth — threshold-free vs threshold-dependent is a second dimension of researcher degrees of freedom beyond the crossing_rate bias.\n\n**Anti-circular-reasoning defense (D031, refined D036)**: The claim that deep layers are \"correct\" for deployment rests on: (1) `crossing_rate` has a MECHANISTIC bias — shallow layers have compressed representations (1.4-2.7× smaller cosine distances) generating noise-driven crossings; (2) threshold-dependent metrics (precision, FPR) directly measure deployment-relevant properties; (3) at deep layers, all 4 models show significant HS advantage (HB corrected); (4) LOO-CV confirms selection stability. The paper presents layer selection as a \"researcher degree of freedom\" with systematic audit via sensitivity analysis.\n\n**Paper treatment**: Dedicated subsection in Method + Table showing metric × model → selected layer + downstream HS-text comparison. Frame as sensitivity analysis, not \"correct vs incorrect.\"\n\n## Story Spine\n\n- **Problem**: Reasoning models exhibit \"self-jailbreak\" — safety reasoning and harmful execution interleave throughout extended CoT, culminating in a \"commitment point\" where safety reasoning permanently ceases. Existing hidden-state detectors (TrajGuard, SafeSwitch, SIREN) target adversarial INPUT attacks on instruction-tuned models; they have never been evaluated on reasoning-model self-jailbreak where the threat is the model's OWN reasoning process.\n\n- **Existing approaches & gaps**: Hidden-state detectors achieve 95% defense on standard LLMs (TrajGuard) but operate at token-level. Reasoning-model safety analysis (Self-Jailbreaking, HarmThoughts) characterizes the phenomenon without providing deployable detectors. Text-level CoT monitors (SafeRemind, SafeThink) are vulnerable to pathological CoT. No prior work systematically compares detection modalities for reasoning-model self-jailbreak.\n\n- **Our framing**: We provide the first systematic evaluation of detection methods for reasoning-model safety commitment across **4 models, 2 architectures (Llama + Qwen), 2 training methods (R1-distilled + STILL), and 2 scales (7-8B + 32B)**, comparing hidden-state probes, text-based probes, sequence models (GRU), change-point detection (BOCPD/CUSUM), unsupervised methods (Mahalanobis, RepE direction, CCS), and nonlinear probes (MLP). We discover that: (a) temporal context is the most robust improvement, benefiting both modalities universally; (b) HS probes universally outperform text at correctly selected layers, with a critical sensitivity to layer selection methodology; (c) existing methods designed for other settings (TrajGuard, BOCPD, CCS, RepE) fail on reasoning model commitment; (d) linear probes are sufficient (MLP adds no value); (e) safety representations are model-specific (cross-transfer fails entirely), validated by control task (selectivity 7.5-18.5pp).\n\n- **Headline result (D032)**: \"Hidden-state probes significantly outperform text probes for safety commitment detection on all 4 models (12/12 Holm-Bonferroni, precision +15-24pp), but this advantage is entirely invisible under biased layer selection metrics. The composite metric `bal_acc × crossing_rate` systematically selects shallow layers at 32B scale, masking the HS signal and producing false negatives. This methodological finding, together with model-specific practical trade-offs at 32B (early detection vs broad coverage) and the comprehensive failure of 6 alternative methods, establishes that supervised temporal probing with principled layer selection is the most effective and robust approach for monitoring reasoning-model safety commitment.\"\n\n## Key Evidence Summary\n\n### R1-8B (Llama-8B, R1-distilled, n=43 test, class_weight=balanced, 60/20/20 split — CANONICAL)\n\nBest layer: 14 (45% depth). Full sweep layers 0-24 confirmed: layer 12 marginally better (+0.4pp bal_acc), no practical difference.\n\n| Method | Coverage | Lead Time | Pre-CP FPR | Safe FPR |\n|--------|----------|-----------|------------|----------|\n| HS Static LR L14 | — | -2.0 | **9.7%** | — |\n| HS Temporal W=5 | — | +0.6 | **10.2%** | — |\n| HS Temporal W=15 | — | **-0.1** | **8.8%** | — |\n| Text Temporal W=15 | — | +10.3* | 20.5% | — |\n| GRU Multi-7L (3-seed) | 72.9+-4.0% | +4.5+-3.0 | 16.5+-4.1% | — |\n\n*Text lead time inflated by high pre-CP FPR (trigger-happy detection)\nNOTE: HS LR lead time collapsed to ~0 with balanced weights — HS detects AT commitment, not before. Only GRU achieves positive lead (+4.5, worst seed +0.9).\n\n### OT-7B (Qwen-7B, STILL-trained, n=47 test, 228 total traces) — D026 CORRECTED to layer 16\n\nBest layer: **16 (57% depth)**. M001 corrected: layer 0 was selected by bal_acc x crossing_rate bug; layer 16 selected by bal_acc.\n\n| Method | Bal Acc | Precision | Coverage | Pre-CP FPR | Lead |\n|--------|---------|-----------|----------|-----------|------|\n| HS Temporal W=3 L16 (best) | **68.8%** | **46.6%** | 53.2% | **10.0%** | — |\n| Text Temporal W=25 (best) | 61.4% | 32.9% | 66.0% | 16.1% | 45.5 |\n| Text Temporal W=3 (matched) | 57.9% | 25.4% | — | 27.1% | — |\n\nBootstrap (layer 16, matched W=3, 1000 resamples):\n   bal_acc +10.7pp, 95% CI [4.9, 16.3], p<0.001 ✅\n   precision +21.9pp, 95% CI [14.0, 29.6], p<0.001 ✅\n   FPR -17.1pp, 95% CI [-22.2, -12.2], p<0.001 ✅ — **3/3 Holm-Bonferroni**\nSelectivity: 18.3pp (real=0.688, random=0.505) — highest across all models\n\n### 32B Cross-Architecture — D029 CORRECTED LAYERS\n\n**QwQ-32B (Qwen-32B, native, n=19-20, Layer 60 — corrected from L2)**\n- HS vs Text bootstrap (null-centered, 1000 resamples):\n  - Bal Acc: +11-22pp across W=1-15, ALL 15/15 significant (p<0.005)\n  - W=3 (matched): bal_acc +15.4pp (p<0.001 ✅), precision +23.6pp (p<0.001 ✅), FPR -9.8pp (p=0.004 ✅) — **3/3 Holm-Bonferroni**\n- Caveats: HS lead time poor (-1.2 to 1.9 steps vs text 3.7-12.3), position baseline competitive\n- Layer selection: `bal_acc × crossing_rate` selected L2 (3.1%); threshold-dependent metrics consensus selects L60 (94%); pure `bal_acc` selects L30 (47%, bal_acc=0.7513 vs L60=0.7101) (D036)\n\n**R1-32B (Qwen-32B, R1-distilled, n=20, Layer 63 — corrected from L10)**\n- W=1: bal_acc +18.7pp, p<0.001 ✅\n- W≥5: NS (dimensionality: 51200 × W features for n=20)\n- W=1: 3/3 Holm-Bonferroni ✅; W=3: 0/3 Bonferroni (null-centered v2); W≥5: NS\n- Layer selection: `bal_acc × crossing_rate` selected L10 (15.6%); threshold-dependent metrics consensus selects L63 (98%); pure `bal_acc` selects L47 (73%, bal_acc=0.8290 vs L63=0.7585) (D036)\n\n**OLD biased-layer results (RETRACTED as metric artifact):**\n| Model | Biased Layer | Corrected Layer | Biased p-value | Corrected p-value |\n|-------|-------------|----------------|----------------|-------------------|\n| R1-32B | L10 (16%) | L63 (98%) | 0.064-0.13 (NS) | <0.001 (W=1) ✅ |\n| QwQ-32B | L2 (3%) | L60 (94%) | 0.35-0.83 (NS) | <0.005 (all W) ✅ |\n\n### 4-Model Holm-Bonferroni Summary (D032 — COMPLETE: 12/12 ✅)\n\n| Model | Arch | Training | Scale | Layer | W | bal_acc | precision | FPR | Selectivity |\n|-------|------|----------|-------|-------|---|--------|-----------|-----|-------------|\n| R1-8B | Llama | R1-distilled | 8B | L14 (45%) | 15 | +8.2pp ✅ | +22.4pp ✅ | -7.4pp ✅ | 12.5pp |\n| OT-7B | Qwen | STILL | 7B | L16 (57%) | 3 | +10.7pp ✅ | +21.9pp ✅ | -17.1pp ✅ | 18.3pp |\n| QwQ-32B | Qwen | native | 32B | L60 (94%) | 3 | +15.4pp ✅ | +23.6pp ✅ | -9.8pp ✅ | 15.3pp |\n| R1-32B | Qwen | R1-distilled | 32B | L63 (98%) | 1 | +18.7pp ✅ | +15.4pp ✅ | -14.6pp ✅ | 7.5pp |\n\n**12/12 all pass Holm-Bonferroni step-down (α=0.05)**. Previously 5/12 with biased layers.\nPrecision advantage remarkably consistent: +15.4 to +23.6pp across all 4 models.\nCaveats: Different W per model; R1-32B W=1 coverage=25%; QwQ lead time negative.\n\n### Position Mediation Analysis (D038 → D039 → D042 complete)\n\n**Finding**: Position (relative step t/T) is a significant component of probe performance. 3/4 models retain HS advantage after residualization; R1-32B is the exception.\n\n| Model | W | Resid HS BA | Resid Text BA | HS-Text Gap | Gap p | HB |\n|-------|---|------------|--------------|-------------|-------|-----|\n| OT-7B | 3 | 0.609 | 0.540 | **+6.9pp** | <0.001 | ✅ |\n| R1-8B | 15 | 0.669 | 0.605 | **+6.4pp** | <0.001 | ✅ |\n| R1-8B | 3 | 0.571 | 0.534 | **+3.7pp** | <0.001 | ✅ |\n| QwQ-32B | 3 | 0.619 | 0.582 | **+3.7pp** | 0.011 | ✅ |\n| R1-32B | 1 | 0.552 | 0.532 | +1.9pp | 0.914 | ❌ |\n\nPosition-only BA: OT-7B/R1-8B/QwQ ≈ 0.706; R1-32B = 0.686.\n\n**D042 final picture**: HS advantage has two separable components — position-mediated and non-positional. 3/4 models retain significant non-positional HS advantage (HB pass); R1-32B's advantage is almost entirely position-mediated (drop 20.4pp, residualized gap NS). This is consistent with R1-32B's other caveats (W=1 only significant, 25% coverage).\n\n**Key implications for paper**:\n1. Position is a significant component (12-20pp drop) — universal\n2. 3/4 models retain non-positional HS advantage (residualized BA gap +3.7 to +6.9pp, HB pass)\n3. R1-32B is the exception: HS advantage position-mediated, residualized gap NS\n4. Temporal windowing preserves non-positional signal (R1-8B: 24pp drop@W=3 → 16pp@W=15)\n5. Paper framing: \"HS advantage persists after position removal in 3/4 models; R1-32B advantage is position-mediated\"\n\n### 5-Seed Robustness (D041)\n\n| Model | HB Pass Rate | Mean ΔPrec ± std | Mean ΔBA ± std |\n|-------|-------------|------------------|----------------|\n| OT-7B | **5/5** | +20.9 ± 1.3pp | +11.3 ± 1.5pp |\n| R1-32B | **5/5** | +13.7 ± 2.0pp | +11.8 ± 2.4pp |\n| QwQ-32B | **5/5** | +19.9 ± 2.5pp | +11.4 ± 3.2pp |\n| R1-8B | **3/5** | +14.6 ± 4.5pp | +9.4 ± 2.4pp |\n\nR1-8B failures are FPR-specific (seed 123 FPR p=0.262, seed 456 FPR p=0.952). Precision is directionally consistent across all 20 seed-model combinations (always positive). ΔPrec never reverses.\n\n**D041 insight**: Robustness splits by MODEL not SCALE. R1-32B (5/5, ΔPrec std=2.0pp) is more stable than R1-8B (3/5, ΔPrec std=4.5pp). \"7-8B robust / 32B sample-limited\" is factually wrong. R1-32B's absolute precision std (12.8pp) vs paired delta std (2.0pp) — paper must distinguish these.\n\n**Per-model evidence tier (D042)**:\n- **OT-7B**: Cleanest — 5/5 HB, largest residualized gap (+6.9pp), highest selectivity (18.3pp)\n- **QwQ-32B**: Robust — 5/5 HB, residualized gap holds (+3.7pp), but position baseline competitive\n- **R1-8B**: Precision-robust — residualized holds (+3.7-6.4pp), but FPR unstable (3/5 HB)\n- **R1-32B**: Statistically stable (5/5 HB, ΔPrec std=2.0pp) but advantage position-mediated (residualized NS)\n\n**Residualized precision**: Not tested (scope limited to BA per Reviewer recommendation).\n\n### BA-Layer Robustness: Metric Circularity Refuted (D044)\n\nR13 W1 concern: corrected selection uses threshold-dependent metrics (precision, FPR), evaluation also uses precision/FPR — potential circularity. Test: compute HS vs text on BA-selected layers (threshold-free criterion).\n\n| Model | BA-Opt Layer | ΔPrec | ΔBA | ΔFPR | ΔAUROC | All 4 HB? |\n|-------|-------------|-------|-----|------|--------|-----------|\n| R1-8B | L12 | +17.8pp | +7.8pp | -12.9pp | +9.3pp | ✅ |\n| OT-7B | L9 | +25.4pp | +11.7pp | -19.3pp | +14.8pp | ✅ |\n| QwQ-32B | L30 | +18.0pp | +14.8pp | -8.0pp | +17.3pp | ✅ |\n| R1-32B | L47 | +13.7pp | +14.9pp | -23.1pp | +21.1pp | ✅ |\n\n**4/4 models × 4 metrics = 16/16 HB pass.** HS advantage is a genuine modality property, not a layer selection artifact.\n\nKey insight: R1-32B on BA-layer L47 shows ΔPrec +13.7pp and ΔAUROC +21.1pp — stronger than corrected L63 in threshold-free metrics. OT-7B on BA-layer L9 has ΔPrec +25.4pp > corrected L16 (+21.9pp). HS advantage is robust to layer selection criterion.\n\nCaveat: QwQ FPR adj_p=0.041 (marginal, CI close to zero). Other 15/16 tests p≤0.006.\n\n### Post-hoc Power Analysis (D044)\n\n| Model | n (test) | MDE (80% power, α=0.05) |\n|-------|----------|------------------------|\n| OT-7B | 47-69 | 6.7-8.1pp |\n| R1-8B | 43-63 | 7.4-9.0pp |\n| QwQ-32B | 29 | 9.5pp |\n| R1-32B | 20-30 | 8.6-11.2pp |\n\nAll significant effects (15-24pp) are well above MDE → adequately powered. R1-32B residualized gap (+1.9pp) is far below MDE (11.2pp) → NS is power limitation, not evidence of absence.\n\n### Negative Results (expanded with R3 supplementary experiments)\n- **BOCPD/CUSUM (4/4 models)**: R1-8B BOCPD 93% detection but lead +3.5 < threshold +4.9; OT-7B/R1-32B/QwQ all 0% detection across all λ. CUSUM worse than threshold on all models.\n- **Mahalanobis (4/4 models confirmed negative)**: R1-8B (+0.07), OT-7B (+0.031), R1-32B (+0.148), QwQ (+0.204) — all separation ≈ 0. Signal in minor PCA components, not principal directions.\n- **RepE direction probing (4/4 models, corrected layers confirmed)**: Direction-based supervised method (needs CP labels). Results highly layer-dependent and model-specific:\n  - R1-32B L63 (corrected): bal_acc=0.559 (near-random), FPR=49.9% — **direction vector fails at deep layer** where supervised LR excels (0.826). Train-test gap 13.3pp shows poor generalization.\n  - QwQ L60 (corrected): bal_acc=0.756, precision=0.512, FPR=0.309 — **improves** from L2 (0.666), narrows gap with supervised LR W=1 (0.740 bal_acc), but still inferior in precision/FPR.\n  - R1-8B L14: bal_acc=0.749, OT-7B L16: bal_acc=0.642\n  - **Key insight**: Supervised LR is necessary — mean-difference direction cannot decode safety commitment at deep layers for R1-32B (discriminative training finds signal that simple direction averaging misses). Layer selection sensitivity applies to RepE too, but in model-specific ways (R1-32B: shallow better for RepE; QwQ: deep better for both).\n- **MLP nonlinear probe (4/4 models)**: 3/4 models worse than LR. Linear probes sufficient; nonlinearity adds no value.\n- **CCS-adapted (Burns et al. 2023, 4/4 models)**: WEAKLY SUPERVISED (needs CP labels for pair construction, not truly unsupervised). R1-8B bal_acc=0.777 (between supervised LR 0.817 and RepE 0.749). R1-32B FPR=0.410 (near-chance). OT-7B precision=0.319 (near-chance, high lead=20.5 is artifact of high FPR). Uses scheme B layers (biased). Paper must: (a) label as \"weakly supervised\" not \"unsupervised\"; (b) disclose pair construction differs from Burns et al.; (c) note scheme B layer limitation.\n- **Cross-model transfer (exp-003)**: All directions fail entirely (best: 58.3% acc, 40.7% crossing).\n\n## Venue Fit Risks\n\n- **EMNLP LLM Security track fit**: Strong — defense/detection evaluation for reasoning model jailbreak.\n- **Method simplicity risk**: Mitigate: frame as empirical study. Contribution is systematic evaluation + layer selection sensitivity discovery + comprehensive negative results.\n- **Statistical power**: With corrected layers, **12/12** tests pass Holm-Bonferroni across ALL 4 models (up from 5/12 with biased layers).\n- **HS advantage universal**: UPGRADED from \"scale-dependent\" — layer selection is the true moderator, not scale. Cleaner story.\n- **Trace vs step FPR**: Both reported with explanation.\n- **High safe FPR risk**: All methods 39-62% safe FPR. Frame as diagnostic tool limitation.\n- **32B caveats**: HS advantage exists but is more sensitive to window size (R1-32B W=1 only) and affected by dimensionality (51200-d at large W with n=20). Lead times shorter than 7-8B. These are honest limitations, not disqualifiers.\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n\n1. \"Method novelty?\" → Empirical study + systematic method comparison + layer selection sensitivity as methodological contribution + comprehensive negative results (6 methods fail).\n2. \"Only one model family?\" → **RESOLVED**: 4 models, 2 architectures (Llama + Qwen), 2 training methods, 2 scales. ALL showing significant HS advantage at corrected layers.\n3. \"GRU unstable?\" → 3-seed reporting with worst-seed bounds. R1-32B GRU at L10 was unstable; needs re-evaluation at L63.\n4. \"Too few traces?\" → Cross-model validation + control task selectivity 7.5-18.5pp.\n5. \"Fine-grained taxonomy?\" → Out of scope for current study.\n6. \"HS doesn't beat text?\" → **QUALIFIED REBUTTAL (D031)**: HS outperforms text at correct layers with STRONG evidence at 7-8B (precision +22pp, Holm-Bonf) and QUALIFIED evidence at 32B (R1-32B: significant W=1 but narrow; QwQ: significant all W but late detection). 32B non-significance at OLD layers was artifact. Layer selection sensitivity analysis shows this is a methodological issue, not absence of signal.\n7. \"Commitment point circular?\" → Cross-model consistency + control task selectivity argues against artifact.\n8. \"Missing related work?\" → Expansion to 30+ citations.\n9. \"No FPR analysis?\" → RESOLVED: both trace-level and step-level FPR reported.\n10. \"High safe FPR?\" → Limitation acknowledged.\n11. \"Control task / position confound?\" → RESOLVED (C2, D011): selectivity 7.5-18.5pp, HS FPR < position-only FPR.\n12. \"Multiple testing correction?\" → **RESOLVED**: 12/12 Holm-Bonferroni step-down (α=0.05) across all 4 models (up from 5/12 with biased layers).\n13. \"Trace vs step FPR?\" → Both reported with explanation.\n14. \"Layer selection is just hyperparameter tuning?\" → No — we show a systematic bias in a commonly used composite metric. The ablation across 5 metrics × 4 models is a reproducible methodological finding, not a one-off parameter choice.\n15. \"Why not just use the best layer for each model?\" → We do. The point is that the METRIC for selecting \"best\" matters. Biased metrics produce systematically wrong selections, masking real effects.\n16. \"32B results are marginal / only W=1 significant for R1-32B?\" → Dimensionality constraint (51200 × W > n=20 at W≥5). QwQ L60 significant at ALL W values. R1-32B L63 significant at W=1. Both confirm HS advantage exists; practical deployment would use W=1-3 for 32B models.\n\n## Title Candidates (D031)\n- \"Layer Selection Matters: How Metric Choice Determines Hidden-State Probe Effectiveness for Safety Monitoring\"\n- \"Temporal Probing for Safety Commitment Detection in Reasoning Models: A Comprehensive Evaluation\"\n- \"What Detects Safety Commitment in Reasoning Models? A Systematic Comparison of Probe-Based Methods\"\n- \"Hidden-State vs Text Monitoring for Reasoning Model Safety: Layer Selection as the Missing Variable\"\n","numLines":323,"startLine":1,"totalLines":323}}}
